var tafsirData_38 = {
  "1": "“Ṣãd. By the Quran, full of reminders!” This verse opens a new chapter with a disconnected letter and an oath. The letter “Ṣãd” is one of the isolated letters that appear at the start of several chapters, recited as an individual sound rather than read as a word. After the letter comes an oath, a solemn swearing by the Quran, described as full of reminders. The combination of the mysterious letter and the weighty oath prepares the reader for the significant message that the chapter will deliver.\n\nThe disconnected letter, like others of its kind, has been the subject of various scholarly explanations. Some considered such letters names of the chapters. Others understood them as a form of oath. A widely held view points out that the Quran is composed of the very letters used in Arabic speech, yet the Arabs could not produce anything like it, making the letters a quiet challenge. Many scholars have taken the most cautious position, holding that the complete meaning of these letters is known to Allah, while affirming that they serve a clear purpose for the listener.\n\nThe effect of the letter, as the chapter begins, is to arrest attention. The unfamiliar sound stops the hearer at the door of revelation and invites a posture of openness. Whatever else the letter signifies, it announces that what follows is a communication from God, not ordinary human speech. The letter creates a threshold, a moment of pause before the message unfolds.\n\nThe oath, “by the Quran, full of reminders,” lends weight to the message that follows. In the Quranic style, an oath draws attention to what follows and affirms its importance. By swearing by the Quran, God elevates its status and signals that a significant declaration is coming. The Quran is itself the thing sworn by, indicating its immense value and authority.\n\nThe description of the Quran as “full of reminders” is significant. The Book is not merely a set of commands or a collection of stories; it is full of reminders, calls to remember the truths that human beings tend to forget. The Quran reminds us of God, of our origin and our destination, of the prophets and their messages, of the Hereafter and its realities. The richness of the reminders means that the Book is a continual resource for awakening the forgetful heart and recalling the heedless soul to the awareness of the most important truths.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to enter the chapter with attentiveness and to receive the Quran as the book of reminders it is. It invites us to approach the chapter with the openness that the mysterious letter demands, to take seriously the oath that elevates the Quran, and to allow the reminders the Book contains to do their work of awakening us to the truths we are prone to forget. The verse challenges us to read the Quran not merely for information but for transformation, allowing its reminders to recall us, again and again, to the awareness of God and of the eternal realities that should shape every dimension of our lives.",
  "2": "“Yet the disbelievers are entrenched in arrogance and opposition.” This verse states the condition of the disbelievers in response to the Quran, the book of reminders sworn by in the previous verse. Despite the reminders the Quran contains, the disbelievers are entrenched in arrogance and opposition. The word “entrenched” suggests a settled, deep-rooted condition, not a passing mood but a fixed stance. Their arrogance and their opposition to the truth are established, deep, and resistant to change.\n\nThe significance of the description lies in its identification of the root of disbelief. The disbelievers’ problem is not intellectual, a lack of evidence or understanding, but moral and spiritual: arrogance and opposition. Arrogance lifts a person above the humility that faith requires. Opposition sets a person against the truth rather than for it. The combination produces the entrenched disbelief that the verse describes, a condition rooted not in the head but in the heart.\n\nThe word “arrogance” is key. Arrogance is the great enemy of faith because it refuses to bow. The truth of the Quran demands humility, the acknowledgment that God is God and we are His servants. The arrogant person refuses this acknowledgment. He will not bow, will not submit, will not accept any authority above his own will. The Quran’s reminders, however clear, cannot penetrate an arrogant heart, because the heart has already decided not to receive them.\n\nThe word “opposition” complements arrogance. The disbelievers are not merely neutral toward the truth; they actively oppose it. They set themselves against the Prophet, against the message, against the reminders. The opposition is active and deliberate, a chosen stance of resistance. The combination of arrogance and opposition produces a formidable barrier to faith, a wall that the disbelievers have built around their hearts and that the Quran’s reminders, however powerful, cannot breach from the outside.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a diagnosis of the root of disbelief and a warning about the dangers of arrogance and opposition. It invites us to recognize that disbelief is often rooted not in the intellect but in the will, in the arrogance that refuses to bow and the opposition that resists the truth, and that the remedy is not more evidence but a softened heart. The verse challenges us to examine our own hearts for traces of the arrogance and opposition that entrench the disbelievers, to cultivate the humility that receives the truth, and to allow the Quran’s reminders to penetrate rather than to harden ourselves against them through the pride and resistance that turn the book of reminders into, for the arrogant, a book whose truths are resisted rather than received.",
  "3": "“Imagine how many peoples We destroyed before them, and they cried out when it was too late to escape.” This verse directs attention to the fate of earlier peoples who, like the current disbelievers, rejected the truth. God destroyed many such peoples before. And when the destruction came, they cried out, but it was too late to escape. The cry came after the opportunity had passed, when the destruction was already upon them and the time for repentance had ended.\n\nThe significance of the verse lies in its use of history as a warning. The current disbelievers are not the first to reject the truth; many before them did the same, and many before them were destroyed. The historical examples are meant to wake the current disbelievers from their complacency, to show them that rejection has consequences, and that those consequences have befallen people just like them. The appeal to history turns the past into a forecast of the future for those who persist in rejection.\n\nThe phrase “how many peoples” emphasizes the frequency of the pattern. This was not a rare event but a recurring one. People after people rejected their messengers, and people after people were destroyed. The frequency underscores the consistency of the consequence and the danger of the path the current disbelievers are walking. The many examples leave no excuse: the current disbelievers cannot claim ignorance of what rejection leads to, because the record of history is full of the answer.\n\nThe crying out “when it was too late to escape” captures the tragedy of the scene. The destroyed peoples, when the destruction came, cried out, presumably in regret, in fear, in a desperate desire to escape. But the cry came too late. The opportunity to turn, which had been available throughout their lives, had passed. The destruction was upon them, and no cry, however desperate, could now avert it. The timing of the cry, after the closing of the door, measures the tragedy of a repentance that comes too late.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering reminder of the fate of those who reject the truth and a call to turn before it is too late. It invites us to recognize that rejection has consequences, that history is full of examples of peoples who learned this too late, and that the time to turn is now, while the door remains open. The verse challenges us to heed the warning of history, to turn to God before the opportunity passes, and to avoid the fate of those whose cry came when it was too late to escape, knowing that the same destruction that overtook the earlier peoples awaits all who persist in rejection, and that the only safety lies in turning to the truth while the turning can still save.",
  "4": "“Now, the pagans are astonished that a warner has come to them from among themselves.” This verse describes the reaction of the pagans to the coming of the Prophet. They were astonished that a warner had come to them, and especially that he came from among themselves, from their own people rather than from some distant or supernatural source. The astonishment reveals their assumptions about how a divine messenger should appear, assumptions that the Prophet’s ordinariness violated.\n\nThe significance of the astonishment lies in what it reveals about the pagans’ expectations. They seem to have expected that a messenger from God would be something other than human, perhaps an angel, or at least a person of great worldly status, wealth, or power. The fact that the warner was one of them, an ordinary man from their own community, violated these expectations and produced astonishment. The astonishment was not reverence but offense: how could God choose such a person to be His messenger?\n\nThe phrase “from among themselves” is important. The Prophet was not an outsider but one of the pagans’ own community, known to them, sharing their language and culture. This was, in fact, a mercy: the messenger spoke their language, understood their circumstances, and lived among them as a familiar figure. But the pagans’ astonishment shows that they saw the familiarity as a problem rather than a mercy. They wanted a messenger who would impress them with otherness, and the familiar Prophet failed to meet that desire.\n\nThe reaction reflects a common misunderstanding of how God works. God often chooses the ordinary, the familiar, the lowly, to accomplish His purposes, confounding the expectations of those who look for worldly impressiveness. The choice of an ordinary man from among the people themselves was consistent with God’s wisdom, but it violated the pagans’ assumptions, and their astonishment revealed the gap between God’s wisdom and human expectation.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a revelation of the misunderstanding that often attends God’s choice of messengers and a call to recognize God’s wisdom in choosing the ordinary. It invites us to recognize that God’s messengers may not meet our expectations of impressiveness, that the choice of the familiar and the ordinary is part of God’s wisdom, and that the astonishment that the pagans felt reveals more about their assumptions than about the Prophet’s legitimacy. The verse challenges us to receive God’s messengers regardless of whether they meet our expectations, to recognize the mercy in the familiar, and to honor the ordinary men whom God has chosen to convey His extraordinary message, knowing that God’s wisdom in choosing them exceeds the assumptions of those who expected something else.",
  "5": "“Has he reduced all the gods to One God? Indeed, this is something totally astonishing.” This verse records the pagans’ astonished objection to the Prophet’s message of divine oneness. The message reduced all the gods, the many deities the pagans worshipped, to One God. To the pagans, accustomed to a pantheon, this reduction was astonishing, even offensive. The idea that there was only one God, and that all their other gods were false, violated their entire religious framework.\n\nThe significance of the objection lies in its revelation of the pagans’ polytheistic mindset. The pagans had grown up with many gods, each with a role, a domain, a following. The message that all these gods were nothing and that only one God was real struck at the heart of their religious identity. The astonishment they expressed was the astonishment of people whose foundational assumptions had been challenged. The reduction of many gods to One was, to them, a radical and shocking claim.\n\nThe phrase “reduced all the gods to One God” captures the pagans’ perception of the Prophet’s message. From their perspective, the message took away their many gods and left them with only one. The word “reduced” suggests diminishment, as if the Prophet had taken something rich and varied and made it small and singular. The perception reveals how deeply the pagans valued their polytheism and how threatening the message of oneness was to their religious system.\n\nThe irony of the astonishment is profound. The pagans were astonished at the claim that God is one, as if oneness were a strange and difficult idea. In reality, the oneness of God is the most natural and rational of beliefs, the acknowledgment that the Creator of all is one, just as the creation is one ordered system. The pagans’ astonishment reveals how far their polytheism had taken them from the natural recognition of God’s oneness. What should have been obvious had become, to them, astonishing, so thoroughly had their false beliefs distorted their thinking.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a revelation of the polytheistic mindset and a reminder of the naturalness of the truth of God’s oneness. It invites us to recognize that the oneness of God, however astonishing it may seem to those steeped in polytheism, is the most rational and natural of beliefs, and that the astonishment of the pagans reveals the distortion that false beliefs produce. The verse challenges us to embrace the truth of God’s oneness, to recognize its naturalness and rationality, and to refuse the polytheistic mindset, whether in its ancient or modern forms, that finds the oneness of God astonishing when it should be the most obvious and foundational of all truths.",
  "6": "“The chiefs among them went forth saying, Carry on, and stand firm in devotion to your gods. Certainly this is just a scheme for power.” This verse records the response of the pagan chiefs to the Prophet’s message. The chiefs, the leaders of the community, went forth urging the people to carry on in their traditional worship and to stand firm in devotion to their gods. They dismissed the Prophet’s message as a scheme, an attempt to gain power. The response reveals the vested interests that motivated the opposition to the truth.\n\nThe significance of the response lies in its revelation of the role of the chiefs in the opposition. The chiefs, who held power and influence in the pagan society, had the most to lose from a message that challenged the existing religious order. Their urging of the people to stand firm in the traditional worship was not driven by genuine conviction but by the desire to protect their position. The chiefs recognized that the Prophet’s message, if accepted, would undermine the social and religious structures on which their authority rested, and they mobilized to defend those structures.\n\nThe phrase “stand firm in devotion to your gods” is ironic. The chiefs urged devotion to gods that were false, powerless, and unable to help. The devotion they urged was devotion to nothing, a loyalty to empty idols masquerading as religious faithfulness. The urging reveals how the chiefs used religion as a tool of social control, mobilizing the people’s attachment to their traditional gods in order to resist a message that threatened the chiefs’ power.\n\nThe dismissal of the Prophet’s message as “a scheme for power” is a classic accusation against those who bring challenging truth. Rather than engaging the content of the message, the chiefs attacked the motive of the messenger, attributing his preaching to a desire for power. The accusation allowed them to dismiss the message without considering it, by impugning the character of the one who brought it. The tactic is common wherever vested interests are threatened by truth: attack the messenger to avoid dealing with the message.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a revelation of the role of vested interests in opposition to the truth and a warning about the use of religion as a tool of power. It invites us to recognize that opposition to the truth may be driven not by genuine conviction but by the desire to protect position and influence, and that accusations against the motives of messengers are often tactics to avoid engaging the message. The verse challenges us to examine our own resistance to the truth, to ensure that it is not driven by vested interests, and to engage the message itself rather than dismissing it through attacks on the messenger, knowing that the truth stands or falls on its own merits, not on the motives, real or imagined, of those who bring it.",
  "7": "“We have never heard of this in the previous faith. This is nothing but a fabrication.” This verse records two further objections from the pagan chiefs. First, they claimed that they had never heard of the Prophet’s message in the previous faith, meaning the religion of their ancestors. Second, they dismissed the message as a fabrication, an invention of the Prophet rather than a revelation from God. Both objections served to justify their rejection without engaging the content of the message.\n\nThe significance of the first objection lies in its appeal to tradition. The chiefs argued that the Prophet’s message was novel, that it departed from the faith of their forefathers, and that this novelty was grounds for rejection. The appeal to tradition is a common defense of the status quo: what is new must be wrong because it is not what we have received. The objection reveals the conservatives’ mindset that values inherited belief over investigated truth, that prefers the familiar to the genuine, regardless of whether the familiar is actually true.\n\nThe irony of the objection is that the Prophet’s message was not, in fact, novel in the most important sense. The worship of the One God was the original faith, the religion of the earliest prophets and patriarchs. It was the paganism of the chiefs that was the innovation, a departure from the pure monotheism of their ancestors. The chiefs, appealing to tradition against the Prophet, were actually defending a recent corruption against an ancient truth. Their appeal to the “previous faith” was itself based on a misunderstanding of what the previous faith had actually been.\n\nThe second objection, dismissing the message as “a fabrication,” is the same accusation that has been leveled against prophets throughout history. Rather than considering whether the message might be true, the chiefs simply labeled it an invention. The label allowed them to reject it without examination. The tactic is convenient: by defining the message as a fabrication in advance, the chiefs placed themselves in a position where no amount of evidence would convince them, because they had already decided what the message was.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a revelation of the tactics used to dismiss challenging truth and a warning about the misuse of tradition and the ease of labeling. It invites us to recognize that appeals to tradition may defend corruption against truth, that labels like “fabrication” may be tactics to avoid engagement, and that the test of a message is its content, not its novelty or its conformity to inherited belief. The verse challenges us to examine the messages we receive on their own merits, to test tradition against truth rather than truth against tradition, and to refuse the convenient dismissals that allow us to reject without considering, knowing that the truth may be older than the traditions that oppose it and that the genuine may be dismissed as fabricated by those whose interests the genuine threatens.",
  "8": "“Has the Reminder been revealed only to him out of all of us?” This verse records another objection from the pagan chiefs. They questioned why the Reminder, the revelation, had been given to the Prophet and not to one of them. The objection reveals their pride and their sense of entitlement. They could not accept that God would choose an ordinary man, rather than one of their elite, to receive His revelation. The objection was driven by wounded pride rather than by genuine inquiry.\n\nThe significance of the objection lies in its revelation of the chiefs’ assumption about how God should work. They assumed that if God were to send a revelation, He would send it to one of the elite, one of the wealthy, the powerful, the influential. The choice of the Prophet, who was not among their elite, offended their sense of how things should be. The objection reveals how deeply their assumptions about status and worth were tied to worldly measures of importance.\n\nThe objection also served as a way to dismiss the Prophet’s message without engaging it. If the chiefs could cast doubt on the choice of the messenger, they could cast doubt on the message itself. By questioning why the revelation had come to him rather than to them, they implied that the revelation was not really from God, because God, in their view, would have chosen more wisely. The objection was thus both an expression of pride and a tactic of dismissal.\n\n“In fact, they are only in doubt of My revealed Reminder.” The verse then exposes the real issue. The chiefs’ objection about the choice of messenger masked a deeper problem: doubt about the Reminder itself. Their questioning of the messenger was a symptom; their doubt about the revelation was the disease. They did not really want the Reminder given to them instead; they did not want the Reminder at all. The objection about the messenger was a cover for the rejection of the message.\n\n“In fact, they do so because they have not yet tasted My punishment.” The verse adds a final explanation: the chiefs’ bold rejection stemmed from the fact that they had not yet experienced God’s punishment. Their security, their ease, their freedom from consequence, allowed them to reject with impunity, at least for the present. The punishment they had not tasted gave them a false confidence, a sense that they could reject without consequence. The explanation warns that the punishment they have not tasted is coming, and that when it arrives, their bold rejection will turn to desperate regret.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an exposure of the pride and doubt that underlie rejection of the truth and a warning about the false confidence that comes from delayed consequence. It invites us to recognize that objections to God’s choice of messengers often mask deeper doubt about God’s message, and that the freedom from immediate consequence that allows bold rejection is temporary and deceptive. The verse challenges us to examine our own responses to God’s revelation, to ensure that we are not masking doubt with objections, and to recognize that the punishment we have not yet tasted does not prove its absence but only the patience of the One who sends it, and that the time to turn is now, before the punishment that has not yet been tasted arrives and turns bold rejection into helpless regret.",
  "9": "“Or is it because they possess the treasuries of the mercy of your Lord, the Almighty, the Giver of all bounties.” This verse poses a searching question to the disbelievers who act with such confidence and independence. It asks whether they possess the treasuries of God’s mercy, the storehouses from which His bounties are distributed. The question expects the answer no. The disbelievers do not possess these treasuries; they are entirely dependent on God for every mercy they enjoy. Their confidence and independence are therefore groundless, resting on nothing but their own illusion of self-sufficiency.\n\nThe significance of the question lies in its exposure of the disbelievers’ true position. They acted as if they were self-sufficient, as if they controlled their own destiny, as if they were not dependent on God for anything. The question strips away this illusion by pointing to the source of every good thing they enjoyed: the treasuries of God’s mercy, which they did not possess and could not control. Every mercy they had, every bounty they enjoyed, came from a source outside themselves, a source they could neither command nor claim as their own.\n\nThe phrase “the treasuries of the mercy” is a beautiful image. God’s mercy is pictured as stored in treasuries, vast storehouses of goodness, ready to be distributed according to His will. The image conveys both the abundance of God’s mercy, there are treasuries of it, and the sovereignty of its distribution, it is given by God, not possessed or controlled by creatures. The disbelievers, who enjoyed the contents of these treasuries without acknowledging the Treasurer, were living on borrowed bounty, sustained by a mercy they refused to recognize.\n\nThe identification of God as “the Almighty, the Giver of all bounties” reinforces the point. He is Almighty, possessing all power, which means that the treasuries are His to give as He wills, and none can compel Him. And He is the Giver of all bounties, the source of every good thing, which means that every mercy the disbelievers enjoyed came from His hand. The combination of almightiness and generosity establishes God’s complete sovereignty over His mercy and the disbelievers’ complete dependence on His giving.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a searching exposure of the illusion of self-sufficiency and a call to acknowledge God as the source of every mercy. It invites us to recognize that we do not possess the treasuries of God’s mercy, that every good thing we enjoy comes from His hand, and that our confidence and independence are groundless apart from the acknowledgment of our dependence. The verse challenges us to give up the illusion of self-sufficiency, to recognize God as the Giver of every bounty, and to live in grateful dependence on the One whose treasuries of mercy sustain us, knowing that the mercies we enjoy are not our possessions but His gifts, and that the acknowledgment of the Giver is the only fitting response to the bounty we have received.",
  "10": "“Or is it because the kingdom of the heavens and the earth and everything in between belongs to them? Let them then climb their way to heaven, if their claim is true.” This verse poses another searching question to the confident disbelievers. It asks whether the kingdom of the heavens and the earth belongs to them. The question expects the answer no. The disbelievers do not own the universe; they possess no kingdom in the heavens or the earth. Their confidence and authority are limited to a small sphere, and even there they are dependent on God. The question exposes the groundlessness of their pretensions.\n\nThe significance of the question lies in its contrast between the disbelievers’ actual position and their pretensions. The disbelievers acted as if they were masters of their destiny, as if they controlled the world, as if their authority were supreme. The question points out that they possess no kingdom, that the heavens and the earth do not belong to them, and that their authority is a small, dependent thing, granted by God and revocable at His will. The contrast exposes the absurdity of their pride.\n\nThe challenge, “let them then climb their way to heaven, if their claim is true,” is a powerful rhetorical move. If the disbelievers really possess a kingdom, let them demonstrate it by ascending to heaven, by exercising authority over the realms they claim to own. The challenge is impossible to meet. The disbelievers cannot climb to heaven; they are earthbound creatures, limited in power and reach, unable to ascend even a little way into the heavens they pretend to rule. The impossibility of the challenge exposes the emptiness of their claim.\n\nThe phrase “the kingdom of the heavens and the earth and everything in between” describes the entirety of the cosmos. This vast kingdom does not belong to the disbelievers; it belongs to God. The disbelievers are not kings of the universe but creatures within it, dependent on the One who is the true King. The recognition of the true ownership of the kingdom should produce humility, not pride, and gratitude, not rebellion. The disbelievers’ failure to recognize the true King led them to pretend to a kingdom that was not theirs.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful exposure of the groundlessness of human pride and a call to recognize God as the true King of the cosmos. It invites us to recognize that we possess no kingdom, that the heavens and the earth belong to God, and that our authority, however real within its sphere, is limited, dependent, and revocable. The verse challenges us to give up the pretension of sovereignty, to acknowledge the true King, and to live in humble dependence on the One whose kingdom encompasses the heavens and the earth and everything in between, knowing that the pride that pretends to a kingdom we do not own is as absurd as the attempt to climb to heaven, and that the only fitting posture before the true King is the humility of creatures who possess nothing that was not given.",
  "11": "“This is just another enemy force bound for defeat out there.” This verse describes the disbelievers who oppose the Prophet and his message. They are an enemy force, but a defeated one, bound for defeat. The description places them within a long line of enemy forces that have opposed God’s messengers throughout history, all of which have been defeated. The current disbelievers, however formidable they may appear, are just another in this series, and their fate will be the same as that of their predecessors.\n\nThe significance of the description lies in its framing of the disbelievers within the pattern of history. They are not a unique or unprecedented threat but just another enemy force, one among many that have risen against the truth. The framing diminishes their apparent power by placing them in a series whose outcome is already known: every enemy force that has opposed God’s messengers has been defeated, and this one will be no different. The description offers reassurance to the Prophet and the believers by reminding them that the current opposition, however fierce, is destined for the same defeat as all previous opposition.\n\nThe phrase “bound for defeat” announces the certain outcome. The defeat is not a possibility but a destiny. The disbelievers are bound for it, headed toward it, unable to avoid it. The certainty of the defeat rests on the pattern of history, in which every enemy force has fallen, and on the decree of God, who has determined the victory of His messengers. The binding for defeat is as sure as the decrees that have governed the fate of every previous enemy force.\n\nThe phrase “out there” is a vivid touch. The enemy force is out there, present, active, opposing. The description does not deny the reality or the presence of the opposition. The enemy force is real, and its opposition is felt. But the reality of the opposition does not change the certainty of its defeat. The enemy may be out there, but it is bound for defeat, and its presence, however threatening, is temporary. The combination of present reality and certain destiny provides a balanced view: the opposition is real, but its end is sure.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reassurance of the certain defeat of the forces that oppose the truth and a call to view present opposition in the light of its destined end. It invites us to recognize that the enemies of the truth, however formidable they appear, are bound for defeat, that history testifies to the certainty of this outcome, and that the present opposition, however real, is temporary. The verse challenges us to take courage in the face of opposition, to view the enemy forces through the lens of their destined defeat, and to stand firm with the truth, knowing that the same pattern that has governed the fate of every previous enemy force governs the fate of all opposition to the truth, and that the defeat of the enemies is as certain as the victory of the messengers whom God has promised to help.",
  "12": "“Before them, the people of Noah denied the truth, as did ’Âd, Pharaoh of the mighty structures.” This verse begins a list of earlier peoples who denied the truth, placing the current disbelievers in a long line of deniers. The people of Noah denied, and so did ’Âd, and so did Pharaoh, the builder of mighty structures. Each of these was a powerful, significant people, and each denied the truth brought to them by God’s messengers. The list establishes the pattern that the current disbelievers are following.\n\nThe significance of the list lies in its use of historical examples to warn the current disbelievers. The peoples named were not weak or insignificant; they were powerful, established, impressive. The people of Noah had flourished before the Flood. ’Âd was known for great physical strength. Pharaoh was the ruler of Egypt, the builder of pyramids and other mighty structures, the leader of a great civilization. The power and the accomplishments of these peoples did not save them from the consequences of their denial. The examples warn the current disbelievers that power and accomplishment are no protection against the fate that denial brings.\n\nThe mention of Pharaoh as “of the mighty structures” is a vivid detail. The Egyptian pyramids and monuments were among the most impressive structures of the ancient world, symbols of Pharaoh’s power and ambition. The detail highlights the magnitude of what Pharaoh built and, by implication, the magnitude of his fall. The builder of mighty structures was destroyed, along with all he had built. The detail underscores the futility of worldly power and accomplishment in the face of God’s decree.\n\nThe phrase “denied the truth” identifies the common fault of all the peoples named. Despite their differences in time, place, and circumstance, they shared the same fundamental error: they denied the truth that God’s messengers brought to them. The common fault produced the common fate: all were destroyed. The consistency of the fault and the fate establishes a pattern that the current disbelievers, who share the fault, will share the fate, unless they turn.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful use of historical examples to warn against denial and a reminder that worldly power provides no protection against the consequences of rejecting the truth. It invites us to recognize that the peoples named were great and powerful, that their greatness did not save them, and that the current deniers, who share their fault, will share their fate unless they turn. The verse challenges us to heed the warning of history, to avoid the denial that destroyed such powerful peoples, and to receive the truth while the receiving can still save, knowing that the same decree that overthrew the people of Noah, ’Âd, and Pharaoh awaits all who follow their path of denial, however great their power or impressive their structures.",
  "13": "“Thamûd, the people of Lot, and the residents of the Forest. These were all enemy forces.” This verse completes the list of earlier peoples who denied the truth, begun in the previous verse. To the people of Noah, ’Âd, and Pharaoh, it adds Thamûd, the people of Lot, and the residents of the Forest. All of these, the verse states, were enemy forces, peoples that set themselves against God and His messengers, and that were destroyed as a consequence.\n\nThe significance of the completed list lies in its breadth and variety. The peoples named represent a wide range of times, places, and circumstances. Thamûd was known for carving dwellings from mountains. The people of Lot were destroyed for their grievous sins. The residents of the Forest, traditionally identified with the people of Shu‘ayb, had their own distinctive characteristics and sins. The variety of the examples demonstrates that denial is not limited to one type of people or one era but has appeared across many different societies. The breadth of the list underscores the universality of the pattern and the relevance of the warning to every people.\n\nThe phrase “these were all enemy forces” gathers the diverse peoples into a single category. Despite their differences, they shared the same fundamental identity: they were enemy forces, opposed to God and His messengers. The common identity produced the common fate: all were destroyed. The gathering of the diverse into the common category warns that any people, regardless of its distinctive characteristics, that takes the stance of an enemy force toward God’s truth will share the fate of all enemy forces.\n\nThe connection between this verse and the previous one is clear. Together, they present a comprehensive list of the destroyed peoples, establishing beyond doubt the pattern of denial and destruction. The current disbelievers, who are also an enemy force, are placed within this pattern, warned by the fate of their predecessors that their own fate will be the same unless they turn. The comprehensiveness of the list leaves no room for the current disbelievers to imagine that they are an exception. The pattern is established, and the warning is clear.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a comprehensive catalogue of historical warnings and a call to take seriously the fate of the peoples who denied before us. It invites us to recognize that denial has appeared in many different societies, that its consequence has been the same in every case, and that the current deniers, like their predecessors, are enemy forces bound for the same defeat. The verse challenges us to heed the comprehensive warning of history, to ensure that we are not numbered among the enemy forces, and to receive the truth while the receiving can still save, knowing that the catalogue of destroyed peoples stands as a permanent testimony to the fate of all who set themselves against God and His messengers, however varied their circumstances or distinctive their characteristics.",
  "14": "“Each rejected their messenger, so My punishment was justified.” This verse states the common fault of all the peoples just listed and the justification of the punishment that overtook them. Each of them rejected their messenger. Despite having a messenger sent to them, despite receiving clear warning, they refused. The rejection was the fault; the punishment was the consequence. And the punishment was justified, fully deserved, because each people had been given the opportunity to believe and had chosen to reject.\n\nThe significance of the verse lies in its establishment of the justice of the punishment. The punishment was not arbitrary or unfair but justified, earned by the rejection that preceded it. Each people had received a messenger, a clear, divinely-sent warner. Each had the opportunity to believe, to heed the warning, to turn to God. Each chose to reject. The rejection, in the face of such clear opportunity, fully justified the punishment that followed. The justice of the punishment rests on the completeness of the opportunity that was refused.\n\nThe phrase “each rejected their messenger” emphasizes the universality of the fault. Not some, but each, every one of the listed peoples, rejected the messenger sent to them. The universality underscores the consistency of human resistance to the truth. The messengers came, one after another, to people after people, and the response, in each case, was rejection. The consistency reveals something deep about the human tendency to resist the truth when it challenges established ways and cherished sins.\n\nThe word “so” connects the rejection to the punishment. The rejection was the cause; the punishment was the effect. The connection is causal and just: the punishment followed from the rejection as a consequence follows from a choice. The connection establishes the fairness of the arrangement: those who are punished are punished for their own choice, the choice to reject the messenger sent to them. No one is punished without having first had the opportunity to believe and having chosen to refuse.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a clear statement of the justice of divine punishment and a warning about the consequences of rejecting God’s messengers. It invites us to recognize that the punishment of the earlier peoples was justified by their rejection, that the same justice governs the fate of all who reject, and that the opportunity we have been given to believe places us in the same position as the peoples who were destroyed. The verse challenges us to receive the messenger God has sent, to heed the warning he brings, and to avoid the fate of those whose rejection justified the punishment that overtook them, knowing that the same justice that punished the earlier peoples for their rejection will punish all who reject, and that the only safety lies in receiving the truth while the receiving can still save us from the justified consequence that refusal will certainly earn.",
  "15": "“These pagans are awaiting nothing but a single Blast that cannot be stopped.” This verse describes the fate awaiting the current pagans. They are awaiting, whether they know it or not, a single Blast that cannot be stopped. The Blast is the sudden, overwhelming event that will overtake them, the consequence of their denial. The description emphasizes both the suddenness and the inevitability of the coming event. It is a single Blast, sudden and decisive, and it cannot be stopped, inevitable and irresistible.\n\nThe significance of the description lies in its revelation of what the pagans are actually awaiting, despite their complacency. They imagine that they are safe, that their denial carries no consequence, that the future will continue as the present. The verse corrects this illusion: they are awaiting a Blast, a sudden, overwhelming event that will shatter their complacency and bring the consequence of their denial. The awaiting is not something they have chosen but something that is coming toward them, regardless of their awareness or their readiness.\n\nThe word “single” emphasizes the suddenness and the decisiveness of the Blast. It will not be a prolonged process or a gradual development but a single event, instantaneous and overwhelming. The singleness of the Blast means that there will be no time to prepare when it comes, no opportunity for last-minute repentance, no chance to escape. The event will arrive in an instant, and its effect will be complete.\n\nThe phrase “that cannot be stopped” emphasizes the inevitability. The Blast is coming, and nothing can prevent it. The pagans’ power, their schemes, their alliances, their denial itself, none of these can stop the Blast from arriving. The inevitability removes any hope of escape through human means. The only escape would have been repentance before the Blast, but once it arrives, it cannot be stopped, and the opportunity for repentance has passed.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering announcement of the certain, sudden consequence that awaits those who deny and a call to prepare before it arrives. It invites us to recognize that the deniers are awaiting a Blast they cannot stop, that their complacency is groundless, and that the same certain consequence awaits all who persist in denial. The verse challenges us to use the present time, before the Blast arrives, to repent and believe, knowing that the event is coming, that it cannot be stopped, and that the only safety lies in turning to God before it comes, while the turning can still save us from the sudden, overwhelming consequence that the deniers, in their complacency, are awaiting without awareness and without preparation.",
  "16": "“They say mockingly, Our Lord! Hasten for us our share of the punishment before the Day of Reckoning.” This verse records the mocking words of the pagans. They dared God to hasten their punishment, calling out, in a tone of mockery, for their “share” to be brought before the Day of Reckoning. The mockery reveals the depth of their disbelief and the folly of their confidence. They treated the punishment as a fiction, something to be called for in jest, rather than as a terrible reality to be feared and avoided.\n\nThe significance of the mockery lies in its revelation of the pagans’ attitude toward the punishment. They did not fear it; they mocked it. They did not believe it was real; they treated it as a joke. The mockery is the ultimate expression of disbelief: not merely denying the punishment but ridiculing the very idea of it. Such mockery reveals a heart so hardened that even the threat of divine punishment produces only laughter, not fear.\n\nThe phrase “our share of the punishment” is a telling detail. The pagans spoke of their share as if it were a portion assigned to them, something they could claim or call for. The language reflects their contemptuous familiarity with the concept, their treatment of the punishment as a trivial matter that could be invoked lightly. The trivialization of the punishment, treating it as a “share” to be hastened, reveals how completely they had lost any sense of its reality or its severity.\n\nThe request to hasten the punishment “before the Day of Reckoning” shows that the pagans knew about the Day, at least in theory. They had heard the warning of a coming Day of judgment. But rather than preparing for it, they mocked it, daring God to bring a portion of the punishment before the Day itself. The daring reveals their confidence that the punishment would not come, that the warning was empty. The confidence was groundless, as the previous verse has shown: they are awaiting a Blast that cannot be stopped. Their mockery does not delay the Blast; it only deepens the guilt of those who mock.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering revelation of the depth of mockery that disbelief can reach and a warning about the folly of treating God’s punishment lightly. It invites us to recognize that the punishment the pagans mocked is real, that their daring was folly, and that mockery of divine warning is a sign of a heart so hardened that it has lost all sense of spiritual reality. The verse challenges us to take seriously the punishment we have been warned about, to avoid the mockery that characterized the pagans, and to use the time before the Day of Reckoning for repentance rather than for ridicule, knowing that the punishment mocked will surely come, and that those who dared it to hasten will find, to their sorrow, that their share was real and that their mockery has only added to the consequence they so lightly invoked.",
  "17": "“Be patient ˹O Prophet˺ with what they say. And remember Our servant, David, the man of strength. Indeed, he ˹constantly˺ turned ˹to Allah˺.”” records David, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records David: or \"isbir 'ala ma yaqooloona wathkur 'abdana Dawooda tha al-aydi innahu awwab,\" and the commentators note David, for the Lord commanded the patience and mentioned David, and the verse is the record of David, the opening that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the patience is the command, and the strength is the praise, and the verse is the record of David, the opening of the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the record is the opening and the patience is the command, and the verse is the record of David, the opening that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the record, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the patience of the prophet is the model, and the one who endures is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who holds the patience is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the record of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the patience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the record is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the prophet: David was the strong and the turning, and the believer who holds the patience is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the record is the teaching that the patience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of David, and the teaching of the patience that the prophet requires.",
  "18": "“We truly subjected the mountains to hymn ˹Our praises˺ along with him in the evening and after sunrise.”” records the subjection, and the commentators note the two parts of the subjection. The verse records the subjection: or \"inna sakhkharna al-jibala ma'ahu yusabbihna bil-'ashiyyi wal-ishraq,\" and the commentators note the subjection, for the Lord subjected the mountains, and the subjection is the gift, the grant of the praise, and the verse is the record of the subjection, the gift that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the subjection is the gift, and the verse is the record of the subjection, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the subjection is the gift and the mountains are the praise, and the verse is the record of the subjection, the gift that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the subjection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the power of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one whom the Lord subjects is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the subjection of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the power is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the subjection is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the mountains were subjected, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the subjection is the teaching that the power is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the subjection, and the teaching of the trust that the power produces.",
  "19": "“And ˹We subjected˺ the birds, flocking together. All turned to him ˹echoing his hymns˺.”” records the birds, and the commentators note the two parts of the birds. The verse records the birds: or \"wat-tayra mahshooratan kullun lahu awwab,\" and the commentators note the birds, for the Lord subjected the birds, and the birds are the gift, the grant of the praise, and the verse is the record of the birds, the gift that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the birds are the gift, and the verse is the record of the birds, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the birds are the gift and the flocking is the praise, and the verse is the record of the birds, the gift that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the birds, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the power of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one whom the Lord subjects is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the birds of the verse are the teaching, the declaration that the power is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the birds is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the birds were subjected, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the birds are the teaching that the power is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the birds, and the teaching of the trust that the power produces.",
  "20": "“We strengthened his kingship, and gave him wisdom and sound judgment.”” records the kingship, and the commentators note the two parts of the kingship. The verse records the kingship: or \"wa shadadna mulkahu wa ataynahu al-hikmata wa fasla al-khitab,\" and the commentators note the kingship, for the Lord strengthened the kingship and granted the wisdom, and the kingship is the gift, the grant of the rule, and the verse is the record of the kingship, the gift that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the kingship is the gift, and the verse is the record of the kingship, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the kingship is the gift and the wisdom is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the kingship, the gift that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the kingship, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own gratitude. The believer is taught that the kingship of the Lord is the gift, and the one who is granted is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the gift is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the kingship of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the kingship is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gift of the Lord: David was granted and judged, and the believer who thanks the gift is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the kingship is the teaching that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the kingship, and the teaching of the gratitude that the gift requires.",
  "21": "“Has the story of the two plaintiffs, who scaled the ˹wall of David’s˺ sanctuary, reached you ˹O Prophet˺?”” opens the story, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse opens the story: or \"wa hal ataka naba'u al-khasmi ith tasawwaroo al-mihrab,\" and the commentators note the opening, for the Lord asked of the plaintiffs, and the opening is the introduction, the beginning of the story, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the plaintiffs are the trial, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the opening is the introduction and the plaintiffs are the trial, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the opening, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reflection. The believer is taught that the story of David is the lesson, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who reads the story is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the opening of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the opening is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reflection of the story: the plaintiffs are the lesson, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the opening is the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the opening, and the teaching of the reflection that the story invites.",
  "22": "“When they came into David’s presence, he was startled by them. They said, “Have no fear. ˹We are merely˺ two in a dispute: one of us has wronged the other. So judge between us with truth—do not go beyond ˹it˺—and guide us to the right way.”” records the entry, and the commentators note the two parts of the entry. The verse records the entry: or \"ith dakhaloo 'ala Dawooda fa-fazi'a minhum qaloo la takhaf khasmani bagha ba'duna 'ala ba'din fahkum baynana bil-haqqi wa la tushtit wahdina ila sawa'i as-sirat,\" and the commentators note the entry, for the plaintiffs entered and asked the judgment, and the entry is the trial, the request of the ruling, and the verse is the record of the entry, the trial that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the request is the trial, and the verse is the record of the entry, the trial of David. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the entry is the trial and the request is the ruling, and the verse is the record of the entry, the trial that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the entry, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own justice. The believer is taught that the request of the judgment is the trial, and the one who is asked is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who judges with the truth is the believer whose justice the Lord accepts, and the entry of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the entry is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the justice of the judgment: David was asked and judged, and the believer who judges with the truth is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the entry is the teaching that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the entry, and the teaching of the justice that the judgment requires.",
  "23": "“This is my brother. He has ninety-nine sheep while I have ˹only˺ one. ˹Still˺ he asked me to give it up to him, overwhelming me with ˹his˺ argument.”” records the case, and the commentators note the two parts of the case. The verse records the case: or \"inna hatha akhee lahu tis'un wa tis'oona na'jatan wa liya na'jatun wahidatun fa-qala akfilneeha wa 'azzanee fi al-khitab,\" and the commentators note the case, for the plaintiff described the case, and the case is the trial, the description of the dispute, and the verse is the record of the case, the trial that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the case is the trial, and the verse is the record of the case, the trial of David. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the case is the trial and the sheep is the dispute, and the verse is the record of the case, the trial that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the case, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own justice. The believer is taught that the hearing of the case is the justice, and the one who hears is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who listens to both is the believer whose justice the Lord accepts, and the case of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the case is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the justice of the hearing: David heard the case, and the believer who listens to both is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the case is the teaching that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the case, and the teaching of the justice that the hearing requires.",
  "24": "“David ˹eventually˺ ruled, “He has definitely wronged you in demanding ˹to add˺ your sheep to his. And certainly many partners wrong each other, except those who believe and do good—but how few are they!” Then David realized that We had tested him so he asked for his Lord’s forgiveness, fell down in prostration, and turned ˹to Him in repentance˺.”” records the ruling, and the commentators note the two parts of the ruling. The verse records the ruling: or \"qala laqad zalamaka bi-su'ali na'jatika ila ni'ajihi wa inna katheeran mina al-khulata'i la-yabghee ba'duhum 'ala ba'din illa allatheena amanoo wa 'amiloo as-salihati wa qaleelun ma hum wa zanna Dawoodu annama fatannahu fastaghfara rabbahu wa kharra raki'an wa anab,\" and the commentators note the ruling, for David judged and then repented, and the ruling is the test, the judgment and the realization, and the verse is the record of the ruling, the test that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the realization is the test, and the verse is the record of the ruling, the test of David. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ruling is the test and the realization is the repentance, and the verse is the record of the ruling, the test that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the ruling, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own repentance. The believer is taught that the judgment of the truth is the justice, and the one who judges is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who repents is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the ruling of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the justice and the repentance are the things that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the ruling is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the justice of the judgment: David judged and repented, and the believer who judges with the truth is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the ruling is the teaching that the justice and the repentance are the things that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the ruling, and the teaching of the justice that the judgment requires.",
  "25": "“So We forgave that for him. And he will indeed have ˹a status of˺ closeness to Us and an honourable destination!”” records the forgiveness, and the commentators note the two parts of the forgiveness. The verse records the forgiveness: or \"fa-ghafarna lahu thalika wa inna lahu 'indana la-zulfa wa husna ma'ab,\" and the commentators note the forgiveness, for the Lord forgave David, and the forgiveness is the mercy, the grant of the closeness, and the verse is the record of the forgiveness, the mercy that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the forgiveness is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the forgiveness, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the forgiveness is the mercy and the closeness is the reward, and the verse is the record of the forgiveness, the mercy that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the forgiveness, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the forgiveness of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who repents is the one whose forgiveness the Lord grants, and the believer who hopes the mercy is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the forgiveness of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the forgiveness is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the Lord: David was forgiven and honoured, and the believer who repents is the believer whose forgiveness the Lord grants, and the forgiveness is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the forgiveness, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "26": "“˹We instructed him:˺ “O David! We have surely made you an authority in the land, so judge between people with truth. And do not follow ˹your˺ desires or they will lead you astray from Allah’s Way. Surely those who go astray from Allah’s Way will suffer a severe punishment for neglecting the Day of Reckoning.”” records the instruction, and the commentators note the two parts of the instruction. The verse records the instruction: or \"ya Dawoodu inna ja'alnaka khaleefatan fi al-ardi fahkum bayna an-nasi bil-haqqi wa la tattabi'i al-hawa fa-yudillaka 'an sabeeli Allahi inna allatheena yadilloona 'an sabeeli Allahi lahum 'athabun shadeedun bima nasoo yawma al-hisab,\" and the commentators note the instruction, for the Lord instructed the justice, and the instruction is the teaching, the command of the judgment, and the verse is the record of the instruction, the teaching that the following verse opens the next account with, and the commentators note the significance, for the justice is the command, and the verse is the record of the instruction, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the instruction is the teaching and the justice is the command, and the verse is the record of the instruction, the teaching that the following verse opens the next account with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the instruction, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own justice. The believer is taught that the judgment of the truth is the justice, and the one who judges is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who avoids the desire is the believer whose justice the Lord accepts, and the instruction of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the instruction is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the justice of the rule: David was instructed and judged, and the believer who judges with the truth is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the instruction is the teaching that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the instruction, and the teaching of the justice that the judgment requires.",
  "27": "“We have not created the heavens and earth and everything in between without purpose, as the disbelievers think.” This verse states a foundational truth about creation: it was not made without purpose. The heavens, the earth, and everything in between were created with intention, with meaning, with a design. The denial of purpose, which the verse attributes to the disbelievers, is a fundamental error that distorts the understanding of everything. Creation is purposeful, and recognizing its purpose is essential to living rightly within it.\n\nThe significance of the statement lies in its confrontation of a common error. The disbelievers, the verse says, think that creation is without purpose. This thinking may take various forms: the belief that the universe is a random accident, that human life has no meaning beyond the physical, that there is no accountability beyond this world. All of these forms share the common error of denying purpose, of treating creation as if it were pointless. The verse confronts this error directly, affirming that creation is purposeful and that the denial of purpose is a mark of disbelief.\n\nThe phrase “without purpose” captures the error precisely. To think of creation as without purpose is to see it as empty of meaning, as a vast mechanism without an intention, as a stage without a play. The error drains creation of its significance, reducing the heavens and the earth to mere matter and motion, and reducing human life to a brief, meaningless flicker between two voids. The verse rejects this bleak vision and affirms the purposefulness of all that God has made.\n\nThe connection between the denial of purpose and disbelief is significant. The verse does not present the denial of purpose as an innocent philosophical position but as a characteristic of disbelief. The disbeliever, having rejected God, finds himself in a purposeless universe, because purpose depends on a Purposer. Having denied the Creator, the disbeliever cannot consistently affirm the purpose of the creation. The denial of purpose is, in a sense, the logical consequence of the denial of God, and the verse identifies the two together.\n\n“So woe to the disbelievers because of the Fire!” The verse then pronounces a warning on the disbelievers. Woe to them, because of the Fire. The warning connects the error of denying purpose with its consequence. Those who live as if creation were purposeless, who act as if there were no accountability, will discover, in the Fire, that creation was deeply purposeful and that their denial of its purpose has led them to a place of severe consequence. The warning is meant to wake the disbelievers from their error before it is too late.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a foundational affirmation of the purposefulness of creation and a warning against the error of denying it. It invites us to recognize that the heavens and the earth were created with purpose, that human life has meaning beyond the physical, and that the denial of purpose is both an error and a danger. The verse challenges us to live in the awareness of creation’s purpose, to seek and to embrace the meaning that God has built into the world, and to avoid the bleak, consequential error of treating creation as if it were without purpose, knowing that the universe is full of meaning because it was made by a God of purpose, and that those who deny the purpose deny, to their own loss, the very reason for which they were made.",
  "28": "“Or should We treat those who believe and do good like those who make mischief throughout the land? Or should We treat the righteous like the wicked?” This verse poses two rhetorical questions that expose the absurdity of treating the righteous and the wicked as if they were the same. The questions expect an emphatic no. God does not treat those who believe and do good like those who make mischief, nor the righteous like the wicked. The two groups are fundamentally different, and their different choices lead to different outcomes. The questions affirm the reality of moral distinction and the justice of differentiated consequence.\n\nThe significance of the questions lies in their affirmation of moral realism. There are real differences between people, based on their choices and their conduct. Those who believe and do good are not the same as those who make mischief. The righteous are not the same as the wicked. The differences are not mere labels or social constructions but real moral distinctions that reflect the actual condition of the hearts and the actual content of the lives of the people involved. The questions affirm that these distinctions matter and that they will be reflected in how God deals with each group.\n\nThe phrase “those who believe and do good” identifies the first group. These are the people whose faith expresses itself in righteous action. They believe, and their belief produces good works. They are the righteous, the people whose lives are marked by devotion and by contribution rather than by harm. The identification is consistent with the Quran’s recurring description of the believers as those who combine faith with action.\n\nThe phrase “those who make mischief throughout the land” identifies the second group. These are the people whose conduct is destructive, who spread corruption and harm rather than good. The mischief-making is comprehensive, throughout the land, suggesting that their harmful influence is widespread and pervasive. The contrast with those who do good is stark: one group contributes to the flourishing of the land, the other to its corruption.\n\nThe two questions, taken together, establish the principle of differentiated consequence. God will not treat the two groups the same. The believers and the mischief-makers will receive different outcomes. The righteous and the wicked will face different destinies. The differentiation reflects the justice of God, who deals with each person according to their choices and their conduct. The questions affirm that the choices we make have consequences, that moral distinctions are real and enduring, and that the final accounting will reflect, with perfect justice, the differences between human lives.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful affirmation of moral realism and of the justice of differentiated consequence. It invites us to recognize that the choices we make place us in one group or the other, that the distinction between the righteous and the wicked is real and will be honored, and that the way we live has eternal significance. The verse challenges us to examine which group we belong to, to ensure that we are among those who believe and do good rather than among those who make mischief, and to live in the awareness that God will not treat the two groups the same, knowing that the differentiation is not arbitrary but just, reflecting the real differences that our choices have made in the kind of people we have become.",
  "29": "“This is a blessed Book which We have revealed to you, O Prophet, so that they may contemplate its verses, and people of reason may be mindful.” This verse describes the Quran and the purpose for which it was revealed. It is a blessed Book, full of divine favor and benefit, revealed to the Prophet. The purpose is twofold: that people may contemplate its verses, and that people of reason may be mindful. The Book is meant to be engaged with deeply, to produce reflection and mindfulness in those who approach it with understanding.\n\nThe significance of the description lies in its identification of the Quran as blessed and in its statement of the Book’s purpose. The blessing of the Book means that it carries divine favor, that engagement with it brings benefit, that it is a source of good for those who receive it. The blessing is not a vague quality but a real feature of the Book, a consequence of its divine origin and its life-giving content. To approach the Book is to approach a source of blessing, a means of receiving the favor that the Book carries.\n\nThe first purpose, “that they may contemplate its verses,” identifies the mode of engagement the Book invites. The Quran is not meant to be read casually or superficially but to be contemplated, studied, reflected upon deeply. The verses are rich with meaning, and their full significance emerges through thoughtful engagement. The call to contemplation suggests that the Book rewards careful, sustained attention, that its depths are plumbed by those who are willing to think deeply about what it says.\n\nThe second purpose, “that people of reason may be mindful,” identifies the outcome the Book aims to produce. Mindfulness, the reverent awareness of God that shapes thought and conduct, is the goal. The Book is meant to produce, in people of reason, this living awareness. The phrase “people of reason” suggests that the mindfulness is not produced by blind acceptance but by thoughtful engagement. The Book appeals to reason, and those who use their reason, who contemplate the verses thoughtfully, will find their mindfulness deepened and their awareness of God strengthened.\n\nThe connection between contemplation and mindfulness is significant. The mindfulness is produced through the contemplation. As people reflect on the verses, they are led into greater awareness of God, of His truth, of the realities that should shape their lives. The contemplation is the means; the mindfulness is the end. The Book is designed to produce, through thoughtful engagement, the reverent awareness that is the foundation of a life lived with God in view.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to engage the Quran deeply and a statement of the purpose such engagement serves. It invites us to recognize the Quran as a blessed Book, to approach it with the contemplation it deserves, and to allow our reflection on its verses to produce in us the mindfulness that is the goal. The verse challenges us to be people of reason, to use our minds in engaging the Book, to contemplate its verses rather than skimming them, and to seek, through our engagement, the deepened awareness of God that the Book is meant to produce, knowing that the blessed Book, contemplated thoughtfully, yields the mindfulness that orders our lives toward the One who revealed it.",
  "30": "“And We blessed David with Solomon—what an excellent servant ˹he was˺! Indeed, he ˹constantly˺ turned ˹to Allah˺.”” records Solomon, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records Solomon: or \"wa wahabna li-Dawooda Sulaymana ni'ma al-'abdu innahu awwab,\" and the commentators note Solomon, for the Lord granted Solomon, and the verse is the record of Solomon, the opening that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the servant is the praise, and the verse is the record of Solomon, the opening of the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the record is the opening and the servant is the praise, and the verse is the record of Solomon, the opening that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the record, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the servant is the praise, and the one who is the servant is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the record of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the record is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the station of the servant: Solomon was the servant, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the record is the teaching that the faith is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of Solomon, and the teaching of the faith that the servant requires.",
  "31": "“˹Remember˺ when the well-trained, swift horses were paraded before him in the evening.”” records the horses, and the commentators note the two parts of the horses. The verse records the horses: or \"ith 'urida 'alayhi bil-'ashiyyi as-safinatu al-jiyad,\" and the commentators note the horses, for the horses were paraded, and the horses are the trial, the display of the world, and the verse is the record of the horses, the trial that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the horses are the trial, and the verse is the record of the horses, the trial of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the horses are the trial and the display is the world, and the verse is the record of the horses, the trial that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the horses, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own gratitude. The believer is taught that the display of the world is the trial, and the one who is tried is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the gift is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the horses of the verse are the teaching, the declaration that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the horses is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trial of the world: the horses were paraded and Solomon remembered, and the believer who thanks the gift is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the horses are the teaching that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the horses, and the teaching of the gratitude that the trial requires.",
  "32": "“He then proclaimed, “I am truly in love with ˹these˺ fine things out of remembrance for Allah,” until they went out of sight.”” records the proclamation, and the commentators note the two parts of the proclamation. The verse records the proclamation: or \"fa-qala innee ahbabtu hubba al-khayri 'an thikri rabbee hatta tawarat bil-hijab,\" and the commentators note the proclamation, for Solomon declared the love, and the proclamation is the teaching, the declaration of the remembrance, and the verse is the record of the proclamation, the teaching that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the love is the remembrance, and the verse is the record of the proclamation, the teaching of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the proclamation is the teaching and the love is the remembrance, and the verse is the record of the proclamation, the teaching that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the proclamation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own gratitude. The believer is taught that the love of the good is the remembrance, and the one who declares is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who remembers the Lord is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the proclamation of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the remembrance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the proclamation is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the remembrance of the good: Solomon declared the love, and the believer who remembers the Lord is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the proclamation is the teaching that the remembrance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the proclamation, and the teaching of the gratitude that the remembrance requires.",
  "33": "“˹He ordered,˺ “Bring them back to me!” Then he began to rub down their legs and necks.”” records the order, and the commentators note the two parts of the order. The verse records the order: or \"ruddooha 'alayya fa-tafiqa mashan bis-sooqi wal-a'naq,\" and the commentators note the order, for Solomon ordered the return and rubbed the horses, and the order is the action, the care of the horses, and the verse is the record of the order, the action that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the order is the action, and the verse is the record of the order, the action of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the order is the action and the rubbing is the care, and the verse is the record of the order, the action that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the order, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own care. The believer is taught that the care of the gift is the duty, and the one who cares is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who tends the gift is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the order of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the care is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the order is the believer whose care the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the care of the gift: Solomon rubbed the horses, and the believer who tends the gift is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the order is the teaching that the care is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the order, and the teaching of the care that the gratitude requires.",
  "34": "“And indeed, We tested Solomon, placing a ˹deformed˺ body on his throne, then he turned ˹to Allah in repentance˺.”” records the test, and the commentators note the two parts of the test. The verse records the test: or \"wa laqad fatanna Sulaymana wa alqayna 'ala kursiyyihi jasadan thumma anab,\" and the commentators note the test, for the Lord tested Solomon, and the test is the trial, the placing of the body, and the verse is the record of the test, the trial that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the test is the trial, and the verse is the record of the test, the trial of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the test is the trial and the body is the sign, and the verse is the record of the test, the trial that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the test, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own repentance. The believer is taught that the test of the Lord is the trial, and the one who is tested is the one whose repentance the Lord values, and the believer who turns to the Lord is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the test of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the repentance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the test is the believer whose repentance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trial of the Lord: Solomon was tested and turned, and the believer who turns to the Lord is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the test is the teaching that the repentance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the test, and the teaching of the repentance that the trial requires.",
  "35": "“He prayed, “My Lord! Forgive me, and grant me an authority that will never be matched by anyone after me. You are indeed the Giver ˹of all bounties˺.”” records the prayer, and the commentators note the two parts of the prayer. The verse records the prayer: or \"qala rabbi ighfir lee wa hab lee mulkan la yanbaghee li-ahadin min ba'dee innaka anta al-wahhab,\" and the commentators note the prayer, for Solomon asked the forgiveness and the authority, and the prayer is the repentance, the request of the grant, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the repentance that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the prayer is the repentance, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the repentance of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the prayer is the repentance and the grant is the request, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the repentance that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the prayer of the believer is the repentance, and the one who asks is the one whose prayer the Lord answers, and the believer who hopes the grant is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the prayer of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the hope is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the prayer is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prayer of the hope: Solomon asked and was granted, and the believer who hopes the grant is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the prayer is the teaching that the hope is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the prayer, and the teaching of the hope that the grant carries.",
  "36": "“So We subjected to him the wind, blowing gently at his command to wherever he pleased.”” records the subjection, and the commentators note the two parts of the subjection. The verse records the subjection: or \"fa-sakhkharna lahu ar-reeha tajree bi-amrihi rukha'an haythu asab,\" and the commentators note the subjection, for the Lord subjected the wind, and the subjection is the gift, the grant of the power, and the verse is the record of the subjection, the gift that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the subjection is the gift, and the verse is the record of the subjection, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the subjection is the gift and the wind is the power, and the verse is the record of the subjection, the gift that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the subjection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the power of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one whom the Lord subjects is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the subjection of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the power is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the subjection is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the wind was subjected, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the subjection is the teaching that the power is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the subjection, and the teaching of the trust that the power produces.",
  "37": "“And ˹We subjected to him˺ every builder and diver of the jinn,” records the jinn, and the commentators note the two parts of the jinn. The verse records the jinn: or \"wash-shayateena kulla banna'in wa ghawwas,\" and the commentators note the jinn, for the Lord subjected the jinn, and the jinn are the gift, the grant of the service, and the verse is the record of the jinn, the gift that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the jinn are the gift, and the verse is the record of the jinn, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the jinn are the gift and the service is the grant, and the verse is the record of the jinn, the gift that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the jinn, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the power of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one whom the Lord subjects is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the jinn of the verse are the teaching, the declaration that the power is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the jinn is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the jinn were subjected, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the jinn are the teaching that the power is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the jinn, and the teaching of the trust that the power produces.",
  "38": "“and others bound together in chains.”” continues the jinn, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse continues the jinn: or \"wa akhareena muqarraneena fi al-asfad,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for the Lord subjected the chained, and the continuation is the gift, the grant of the power, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the gift that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the chained is the gift, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the gift and the chained is the power, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the gift that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the power of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one whom the Lord subjects is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the continuation of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the power is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the chained were subjected, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the continuation is the teaching that the power is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the trust that the power produces.",
  "39": "“˹Allah said,˺ “This is Our gift, so give or withhold ˹as you wish˺, never to be called to account.”” records the gift, and the commentators note the two parts of the gift. The verse records the gift: or \"hatha 'ata'una famnun aw amsik bi-ghayri hisab,\" and the commentators note the gift, for the Lord declared the gift, and the gift is the grant, the declaration of the freedom, and the verse is the record of the gift, the grant that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the gift is the grant, and the verse is the record of the gift, the grant of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the gift is the grant and the freedom is the account, and the verse is the record of the gift, the grant that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the gift, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the gift of the Lord is the grant, and the one who is granted is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the gift of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives, and the believer who reads the gift is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the grant of the Lord: Solomon was granted the gift, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the gift is the teaching that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives. The verse is the record of the gift, and the teaching of the trust that the grant produces.",
  "40": "“And he will indeed have ˹a status of˺ closeness to Us and an honourable destination!”” records the closeness, and the commentators note the two parts of the closeness. The verse records the closeness: or \"wa inna lahu 'indana la-zulfa wa husna ma'ab,\" and the commentators note the closeness, for the Lord granted Solomon the closeness, and the closeness is the reward, the grant of the station, and the verse is the record of the closeness, the reward that the following verse opens the next account with, and the commentators note the significance, for the closeness is the reward, and the verse is the record of the closeness, the reward of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the closeness is the reward and the station is the grant, and the verse is the record of the closeness, the reward that the following verse opens the next account with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the closeness, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the closeness of the Lord is the reward, and the one who is granted is the one whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord grants, and the closeness of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the closeness is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the Lord: Solomon was granted the closeness, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the closeness is the teaching that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the closeness, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "41": "“And remember Our servant Job, when he cried out to his Lord, “Satan has afflicted me with distress and suffering.”” records Job, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records Job: or \"wathkur 'abdana Ayyooba ith nada rabbahu annee massaniya ash-shaytanu bi-nusbin wa 'athab,\" and the commentators note Job, for Job cried the distress, and the cry is the prayer, the recourse of the patient, and the verse is the record of Job, the prayer that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the patience is the prayer, and the verse is the record of Job, the prayer of the patient. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the cry is the prayer and the patience is the recourse, and the verse is the record of Job, the prayer that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the cry, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the patience of Job is the model, and the one who cries is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who endures the trial is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the cry of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the patience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the cry is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of Job: Job cried and was answered, and the believer who endures the trial is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the cry is the teaching that the patience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of Job, and the teaching of the patience that the trial requires.",
  "42": "“˹We responded,˺ “Stomp your foot: ˹now˺ here is a cool ˹and refreshing˺ spring for washing and drinking.”” records the response, and the commentators note the two parts of the response. The verse records the response: or \"urkud bi-rijlika hatha mughtasalun baridun wa sharab,\" and the commentators note the response, for the Lord commanded the stomp and granted the spring, and the response is the mercy, the answer of the prayer, and the verse is the record of the response, the mercy that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the spring is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the response, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the response is the mercy and the spring is the answer, and the verse is the record of the response, the mercy that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the response, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the answer of the prayer is the mercy, and the one who cries is the one whose prayer the Lord answers, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the response of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the response is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the Lord: Job was answered and healed, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the response is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the response, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "43": "“And We gave him back his family, twice as many, as a mercy from Us and a lesson for people of reason.”” records the restoration, and the commentators note the two parts of the restoration. The verse records the restoration: or \"wa wahabna lahu ahlahu wa mithlahum ma'ahum rahmatan minna wa thikra li-uli al-albab,\" and the commentators note the restoration, for the Lord restored the family, and the restoration is the mercy, the grant of the reward, and the verse is the record of the restoration, the mercy that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the restoration is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the restoration, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the restoration is the mercy and the family is the reward, and the verse is the record of the restoration, the mercy that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the restoration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the restoration of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who endures is the one whose reward the Lord grants, and the believer who hopes the reward is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the restoration of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the restoration is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the patience: Job was restored, and the believer who endures is the believer whose reward the Lord grants, and the restoration is the teaching that the reward is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the restoration, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "44": "“˹And We said to him,˺ “Take in your hand a bundle of grass, and strike ˹your wife˺ with it, and do not break your oath.” We truly found him patient. What an excellent servant ˹he was˺! Indeed, he ˹constantly˺ turned ˹to Allah˺.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: or \"wa khuth bi-yadika dighthan fadrib bihi wa la tahnath inna wajadnahu sabiran ni'ma al-'abdu innahu awwab,\" and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded the bundle, and the command is the mercy, the fulfillment of the oath, and the verse is the record of the command, the mercy that the account closes with, and the commentators note the significance, for the patience is the praise, and the verse is the record of the command, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the mercy and the patience is the praise, and the verse is the record of the command, the mercy that the account closes with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the patience of Job is the model, and the one who endures is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who holds the patience is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the command of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the patience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of Job: Job was patient and praised, and the believer who endures is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the patience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the patience that the trial requires.",
  "45": "“And remember Our servants: Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, the men of strength and insight.” This verse introduces a further group of prophets for remembrance: Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. They are identified as God’s servants and described as men of strength and insight. The introduction calls the listener to remember these great patriarchs, to recall their stories and their qualities, and to draw from their example the lessons their lives offer.\n\nThe significance of the introduction lies in its identification of the patriarchs by two key qualities: strength and insight. Strength refers to their capacity to endure, to stand firm, to carry the weight of their prophetic missions. Insight refers to their understanding, their discernment, their ability to perceive the truth and to guide others toward it. The combination of strength and insight presents the patriarchs as complete servants: strong in action and perceptive in understanding. The qualities complement each other, producing a balanced and effective faithfulness.\n\nThe phrase “Our servants” identifies the patriarchs by their fundamental relationship to God. They were servants, living in God’s service, oriented around His will. The identification places them in the company of all the faithful, from Noah to the final prophet, who shared the same identity as servants of the One God. The common identity underscores the unity of the faithful across the generations and the consistency of the calling to which all are summoned.\n\nThe call to “remember” is significant. The patriarchs are not merely named but are to be remembered, recalled, reflected upon. The remembrance is active, not passive: the listener is invited to bring these figures to mind, to consider their lives, to draw lessons from their examples. The call to remembrance reflects the Quran’s use of history as a teacher, presenting the lives of the faithful as models from which the living can learn.\n\nThe grouping of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob reflects their family relationship and the continuity of the prophetic tradition across three generations. Abraham was the patriarch, Isaac his son, Jacob his grandson. The three together represent a lineage of faith, a family in which devotion was transmitted from father to son to grandson. The continuity of faith across the generations is itself a lesson: devotion can be passed on, and the faithful of one generation can become the examples and the ancestors of the faithful of the next.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to remember the patriarchs and to learn from their qualities. It invites us to recognize the value of strength and insight, the complementarity of endurance and understanding, and the importance of the lineage of faith that the patriarchs represent. The verse challenges us to cultivate both strength and insight in our own lives, to remember the examples of the faithful who have gone before, and to consider how our own devotion might, like theirs, be transmitted to the generations that follow, knowing that the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob is the God of all who serve Him, and that the qualities that made these patriarchs great are the qualities to which all the faithful are called.",
  "46": "“We truly chose them for the honour of proclaiming the Hereafter.” This verse states a specific purpose for which God chose the patriarchs: the honour of proclaiming the Hereafter. They were selected, set apart, for the task of declaring the reality of the life to come. The choosing was purposeful: God did not choose them randomly but for a specific mission, the proclamation of the Hereafter to a world inclined to forget or deny it.\n\nThe significance of the choosing lies in its identification of the patriarchs’ mission. They were proclaimers of the Hereafter. Their lives, their messages, their examples were oriented toward declaring the reality of the life to come, the Day of Judgment, the reward of the righteous, the consequence of the wicked. The proclamation of the Hereafter was central to their prophetic work, because the Hereafter is central to the message of all the prophets: the awareness that this life is not all, that there is a reckoning to come, that the choices of the present carry eternal weight.\n\nThe phrase “the honour of proclaiming” identifies the task as an honour. To proclaim the Hereafter was not a burden but a privilege, an honour bestowed on the chosen servants. The honour reflects the importance of the message: the proclamation of the Hereafter is among the most important tasks a servant can undertake, because it alerts human beings to the reality that should shape every choice and every priority. The patriarchs were honoured by being entrusted with this crucial proclamation.\n\nThe word “truly” emphasizes the reality of the choosing. The patriarchs were genuinely, certainly chosen for this purpose. The emphasis underscores the deliberateness of the divine selection and the reality of the mission. The choosing was not a vague sense of calling but a definite, purposeful selection, a setting apart for a specific task. The emphasis invites the listener to take seriously both the choosing and the task for which the choosing occurred.\n\nThe connection between the choosing and the proclamation of the Hereafter reveals something about the nature of prophethood. The prophets were not merely moral teachers or social reformers; they were proclaimers of the eternal, declarers of the reality that lies beyond this life. Their message always included the Hereafter, because the Hereafter is the context that gives this life its full meaning. Without the proclamation of the Hereafter, the prophetic message would be incomplete, lacking the dimension of eternal accountability that completes the call to faith and righteousness.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a revelation of the patriarchs’ mission and a reminder of the centrality of the Hereafter to the prophetic message. It invites us to recognize that the proclamation of the Hereafter is an honour, entrusted to the chosen servants, and that the awareness of the life to come should shape our present choices. The verse challenges us to receive the proclamation that the patriarchs, and all the prophets, offered, to take seriously the reality of the Hereafter, and to live in the awareness that this life is not all, that there is a reckoning to come, and that the choices we make now carry the eternal weight that the patriarchs were honoured to proclaim.",
  "47": "“And in Our sight they are truly among the chosen and the finest.” This verse describes the standing of the patriarchs in God’s sight. They are among the chosen and the finest. The description is one of the highest honor: the patriarchs are not merely servants but chosen servants, not merely good but among the finest. The standing reflects the quality of their lives and the favor in which God held them.\n\nThe significance of the standing lies in its revelation of how God regards His faithful servants. The patriarchs, in God’s sight, are among the chosen and the finest. The standing is not their own assessment but God’s, the verdict of the One whose assessment matters most. The divine approval honors the patriarchs with a recognition that exceeds any human commendation, placing them among the highest of the high in the sight of the One who sees all.\n\nThe phrase “in Our sight” grounds the standing in God’s perspective. The patriarchs are chosen and fine not in the estimation of the world, which may value different qualities, but in the estimation of God, whose judgment is true. The grounding in God’s sight means that the standing is secure, not subject to the fluctuations of human opinion, but fixed by the verdict of the One whose verdict is final. The security of the standing offers comfort to all who serve God: the standing that matters is the standing in God’s sight, and that standing is determined by His just and loving assessment.\n\nThe phrase “the chosen” identifies the patriarchs as selected by God, set apart for His purposes. The choosing, mentioned in the previous verse for the proclamation of the Hereafter, is here presented as an ongoing reality: they are among the chosen, those whom God has selected and whom He holds in special favor. The chosenness is both a privilege and a responsibility, an honor that carries with it the duties of the chosen.\n\nThe phrase “the finest” describes the quality of the patriarchs. They are among the best, the highest, the most excellent. The description reflects the actual quality of their lives: their faith, their devotion, their strength, their insight. The quality earned the standing, and the standing honors the quality. The combination presents the patriarchs as both chosen by grace and fine by character, the two elements held together in the description of their standing.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a revelation of the high standing of the faithful in God’s sight and an encouragement regarding the honor God bestows on His servants. It invites us to recognize that God regards His faithful servants as chosen and fine, that His assessment is the one that matters, and that the standing He grants is secure. The verse challenges us to seek the standing that the patriarchs enjoyed, to live in a way that would be regarded by God as chosen and fine, and to trust that the One who chose and honored the patriarchs chooses and honors all who turn to Him in sincere devotion, granting them a standing in His sight that no human assessment can give or take away.",
  "48": "“Also remember Ishmael, Elisha, and Ⱬul-Kifl. All are among the best.” This verse adds three more prophets to the list of those to be remembered: Ishmael, Elisha, and Ⱬul-Kifl. All three are described as among the best. The addition extends the catalogue of the faithful, gathering more witnesses into the gallery of those whose lives exemplify devotion and whose standing with God is one of honor.\n\nThe significance of the addition lies in its expansion of the list of the faithful. The chapter has already presented Noah, Abraham, Moses, Aaron, Elias, Lot, Jonah, David, Solomon, Job, Isaac, and Jacob. Now it adds Ishmael, Elisha, and Ⱬul-Kifl. The expansion reflects the abundance of God’s faithful servants throughout history and the breadth of the prophetic witness. The faithful are not few but many, drawn from many times and places, and their collective example offers a rich resource for instruction and encouragement.\n\nThe call to “remember” each of these prophets is significant. The remembrance is not mere recollection of names but a thoughtful engagement with their lives and their examples. Each prophet has a story, a mission, a set of qualities that made him faithful. The remembrance brings these to mind and allows the listener to learn from them. The call to remembrance, extended to so many prophets, reflects the Quran’s conviction that the lives of the faithful are a treasury of instruction, available to all who will engage them thoughtfully.\n\nThe description “all are among the best” places the three added prophets in the same high category as the others. They are not lesser figures but among the best, sharing the standing and the quality of the more prominently featured prophets. The description ensures that the listener does not regard them as marginal but recognizes their excellence and their worthiness of remembrance alongside the others.\n\nThe inclusion of Ⱬul-Kifl, a figure about whom less is known with certainty than the others, reflects the Quran’s willingness to honor the faithful even when their stories are not told in detail. The mention by name, with the declaration of being among the best, honors the figure regardless of the listener’s knowledge of his specific story. The inclusion teaches that excellence is honored by God whether or not it is widely known, and that the faithful whose stories are not famous are nonetheless among the best in God’s sight.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an expansion of the gallery of the faithful and a reminder that God’s excellent servants are many. It invites us to remember the prophets named, to learn from their examples, and to recognize that the company of the faithful is large and diverse, drawn from many times and places. The verse challenges us to honor all of God’s faithful servants, whether their stories are famous or little known, to learn from the collective witness of their lives, and to aspire to the standing they enjoy, knowing that excellence, whether widely recognized or known only to God, is honored by the One whose assessment is the only one that finally matters.",
  "49": "“This is all a reminder. And the righteous will certainly have an honourable destination.” This verse identifies the preceding material as a reminder and announces the destination of the righteous. The stories of the prophets, their qualities, their standing, all of this is a reminder, meant to call to mind the truths that human beings tend to forget. And the righteous, those who respond to the reminder with faith and action, will have an honourable destination, a final abode marked by dignity and blessing.\n\nThe significance of the identification lies in its statement of the purpose of the prophetic narratives. The stories are not told for entertainment or for historical interest but as reminders, calls to remember. The reminder functions by bringing to mind the examples of the faithful, the principles of God’s dealing with His servants, and the realities that should shape the listener’s own life. The identification as a reminder gives the narratives a practical, transformative purpose: they are meant to change the listener, to call forth faith and righteousness through the recollection of the faithful who have gone before.\n\nThe phrase “the righteous will certainly have an honourable destination” states the outcome of the life of faith. The righteous, those who live by the truths the reminder presents, will have an honourable destination. The certainty is marked by “certainly.” The destination is honourable, marked by dignity and blessing. The statement offers both assurance and motivation: assurance that righteousness leads to honor, and motivation to pursue righteousness in light of the destination it earns.\n\nThe connection between the reminder and the destination is significant. The reminder presents the examples of the faithful; the destination is the outcome of following those examples. The reminder calls to righteousness; the destination rewards it. The two belong together: the reminder is the means by which the listener is drawn toward the righteousness that leads to the honourable destination. The connection gives the reminder its urgency and its value: it is the pathway, through the examples it presents, to the destination it announces.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an identification of the purpose of the prophetic narratives and an announcement of the destination of the righteous. It invites us to receive the narratives as reminders, to allow them to call us to the truths we may have forgotten, and to pursue the righteousness that leads to the honourable destination. The verse challenges us to take the reminders seriously, to let the examples of the faithful shape our own lives, and to live in the confident hope of the honourable destination that God has prepared for the righteous, knowing that the same destination that awaits the prophets awaits all who walk the path of faith and righteousness that the prophets so faithfully trod.",
  "50": "“The Gardens of Eternity, whose gates will be open for them.” This verse identifies the honourable destination of the righteous as the Gardens of Eternity, and describes a striking feature of these Gardens: their gates will be open. The openness of the gates is a powerful image, conveying welcome, security, and the absence of every barrier. The righteous, arriving at their destination, will find the gates open, an unmistakable sign of their welcome and their belonging.\n\nThe significance of the Gardens of Eternity lies in their identification as the destination of the righteous. The destination is not a vague spiritual state but a specific place, the Gardens of Eternity, described throughout the Quran as places of beauty, delight, and peace. The Gardens are the fulfillment of the hope of the righteous, the concrete realization of the reward that faith and good works earn. The identification of the destination gives the righteous a clear vision of what awaits them, strengthening their hope and their motivation.\n\nThe phrase “Gardens of Eternity” emphasizes both the nature and the duration of the destination. The Gardens are places of beauty, growth, and life, evoking images of lush vegetation, flowing water, and abundant provision. And they are eternal, lasting forever, without end. The combination of beauty and eternity presents a destination that satisfies both the desire for delight and the desire for permanence. The Gardens are beautiful enough to delight forever and eternal enough to make the delight unending.\n\nThe image of the gates being open is rich with meaning. In this world, gates are closed for security, opened only to the welcome or the authorized. The open gates of the Gardens of Eternity suggest a place of perfect security, where there is no threat to guard against, and of perfect welcome, where the righteous belong and are expected. The openness conveys both the absence of danger, the gates do not need to be closed for protection, and the fullness of acceptance, the righteous are not merely admitted but welcomed, with the gates held open for their arrival.\n\nThe openness of the gates also suggests the freedom of the Gardens. The righteous will not be confined or restricted but free, able to come and to go within the blessed realm. The freedom is part of the reward: after the constraints and limitations of earthly life, the righteous enter a place of perfect freedom, where the open gates symbolize the liberty that characterizes their eternal home.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a beautiful picture of the destination of the righteous and a source of hope and motivation. It invites us to recognize that the righteous will enter Gardens of Eternity, that the gates of those Gardens will stand open for them, and that the welcome, the security, and the freedom symbolized by the open gates await all who walk the path of faith. The verse challenges us to pursue the righteousness that leads to this destination, to live in the hope of the open gates, and to trust that the One who has prepared the Gardens of Eternity will welcome us, when we arrive, with gates that stand open in the fullness of His acceptance and the perfection of His love.",
  "51": "“There they will recline, calling for abundant fruit and drink.” This verse describes the condition of the righteous within the Gardens of Eternity. They will recline, in a posture of rest and ease, and they will call for abundant fruit and drink, which will be provided for them. The picture is one of perfect comfort and perfect provision, where every need and every desire is met through effortless abundance.\n\nThe significance of the description lies in its portrayal of the ease and the abundance of Paradise. The righteous will recline, suggesting complete rest, the absence of every strain and every burden. The reclining is the posture of those who have nothing left to strive for, nothing to achieve, nothing to defend. The striving of earthly life, with its labor and its anxiety, is over, replaced by the perfect rest of reclining in the Gardens of Eternity.\n\nThe calling for fruit and drink suggests the ease of the provision. The righteous do not need to labor for their food or to worry about their sustenance. They simply call, and what they desire is brought to them. The ease of the provision contrasts sharply with the toil that marks earthly sustenance, where food and drink must be earned through labor. In Paradise, the labor is over, and the provision is effortless, available for the asking.\n\nThe abundance of the fruit and drink is emphasized. The fruit is not scarce but abundant, plentiful beyond any measure of need. The drink is not rationed but freely available. The abundance reflects the generosity of the Giver and the completeness of the provision. The righteous will not experience scarcity or want of any kind; every good thing will be present in overflowing measure, more than enough to satisfy every desire.\n\nThe detail of the fruit and the drink engages the senses and the appetites, presenting Paradise as a place of genuine, sensory delight. The Quran does not shy away from describing Paradise in terms of physical pleasures, because the human being is a creature of body as well as spirit, and the reward of Paradise addresses the whole person. The fruit and the drink are real delights, suited to the embodied nature of those who will enjoy them, and their abundance reflects the comprehensive nature of the reward.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a vivid picture of the ease and abundance of Paradise and a source of hope for those who walk the path of faith. It invites us to recognize that Paradise is a place of perfect rest and perfect provision, where every need is met and every desire fulfilled, and that the toil and scarcity of earthly life will be replaced by the reclining and the abundance of the Gardens. The verse challenges us to pursue the destination it describes, to endure the toil of faithful living with the hope of the coming rest, and to trust that the One who has prepared the reclining and the abundance will welcome us, when we arrive, into the perfect ease and the overflowing provision that the verse so beautifully portrays.",
  "52": "“And with them will be maidens of modest gaze and equal age.” This verse describes another aspect of the companionship of Paradise: the presence of maidens characterized by modest gaze and equal age. The description, parallel to one found in the previous chapter, presents the companionship of Paradise as marked by both beauty and virtue, by qualities that engage both the eye and the heart. The companionship is part of the comprehensive reward that Paradise offers.\n\nThe significance of the description lies in its presentation of the companionship of Paradise as both beautiful and pure. The maidens are described by qualities that encompass both appearance and character. The modest gaze suggests virtue, restraint, and a beauty that is accompanied by moral dignity. The equal age suggests harmony and suitability, the companions being matched in years and therefore in the harmony of their shared stage. The combination presents a companionship that satisfies on every level.\n\nThe phrase “modest gaze” has appeared before in the Quran’s descriptions of Paradise and carries the same rich meaning. The modesty of the gaze conveys purity, restraint, and a dignity that enhances rather than diminishes the beauty of the one who possesses it. The companionship of Paradise is not characterized by immodesty or by the kind of beauty that is separated from virtue, but by a beauty that is joined to moral quality, a beauty that is both attractive and pure.\n\nThe phrase “equal age” suggests a harmony between the righteous and their companions, a matching that makes the companionship natural and complete. The equality of age implies a shared stage, a common maturity, a suitability that makes the relationship harmonious. The harmony is part of the perfection of Paradise, where every element is suited to every other, where there is no mismatch or discord, but a comprehensive fitting-together of all things.\n\nThe companionship described is part of the Quran’s broader vision of Paradise as a place of comprehensive satisfaction, where every good and legitimate human desire is fulfilled in a purified form. The desire for companionship, for beauty, for harmony, for the pleasures of relationship, is addressed in the description, which presents these desires fulfilled in a context of perfect purity and perfect suitability. The fulfillment reflects the generosity of the God who made human beings with these desires and who has prepared a place where they are met, perfectly and purely, for the joy of the righteous.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a glimpse of the companionship of Paradise and a reminder that the reward of the righteous encompasses every dimension of human well-being. It invites us to recognize that Paradise is a place of comprehensive satisfaction, where beauty, virtue, and harmony are joined in a companionship that fulfills the deepest human desires in a purified form, and that the God who made us with these desires has prepared a place where they are perfectly met. The verse challenges us to pursue the destination it describes, to order our desires in this life toward the purity that Paradise embodies, and to trust that the One who has prepared the companionship of Paradise will welcome us, when we arrive, into the perfect relationships and the pure delights that the verse so beautifully portrays.",
  "53": "“This is what you are promised for the Day of Reckoning.” This verse identifies the preceding description of Paradise as a promise, given for the Day of Reckoning. The Gardens, the reclining, the fruit, the drink, the companionship, all of this is what has been promised, and the promise will be fulfilled on the Day of Reckoning, the great Day of accounting and reward. The identification links the present description to the future fulfillment, assuring the listener that what is described is not a fantasy but a guaranteed reality.\n\nThe significance of the identification lies in its statement of the certainty and the timing of the fulfillment. The description of Paradise is a promise, and the promise is for the Day of Reckoning. The certainty is implied: what God promises, He fulfills. The timing is specified: the Day of Reckoning, the Day when accounts are settled and destinies assigned. The combination of certainty and specified timing gives the description a weight and a relevance that a mere imaginative picture would lack. This is not poetry for its own sake but a promise with a date, anchored in the reality of the coming Day.\n\nThe phrase “this is what you are promised” emphasizes the personal nature of the promise. The description is not a general statement about Paradise but a specific promise to the listeners, to “you.” The personalization makes the promise direct and relevant, applying the description to the very people who hear it. The promise invites each listener to consider whether he or she will be among those for whom the promise is fulfilled, a consideration that should prompt the faith and righteousness that the promise rewards.\n\nThe identification of the Day as “the Day of Reckoning” is significant. The Day is one of reckoning, of accounting, of the settling of all accounts. The promise of Paradise is fulfilled on this Day because it is the Day when the accounting determines who has earned the reward. The Gardens are not given randomly but through the reckoning that assesses each life and assigns its fitting destination. The identification of the Day as one of reckoning underscores the justice of the arrangement: the promise is fulfilled, on the Day of Reckoning, to those whose reckoning qualifies them for it.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an assurance of the certainty of the promise and a reminder of the Day on which it will be fulfilled. It invites us to recognize that the descriptions of Paradise are not fantasies but promises, guaranteed by the One who made them, and that the fulfillment will come on the Day of Reckoning, the Day that determines every destiny. The verse challenges us to receive the promise personally, to consider whether our lives qualify us for its fulfillment, and to live in the faith and righteousness that the Day of Reckoning will recognize and reward, knowing that the promise, however beautiful its description, is more beautiful still in its fulfillment, and that the Day on which it is kept is coming as surely as the word of the One who gave it.",
  "54": "“This is indeed Our provision that will never end.” This verse identifies the Paradise described as God’s provision, and emphasizes its endlessness. The provision is God’s, given by Him, and it will never end. The two qualities, divine source and endless duration, together present a provision that is both perfectly reliable and perfectly lasting, a sustenance that comes from the Giver of all good and that continues without interruption forever.\n\nThe significance of the identification lies in its grounding of the provision in God. The Paradise described is not a natural consequence of human goodness or a self-generated reward but God’s provision, freely given by Him. The grounding in God means that the provision shares the reliability of the Giver: as God is faithful, so the provision is certain; as God is generous, so the provision is abundant; as God is eternal, so the provision is endless. The qualities of the provision flow from the qualities of the Provider.\n\nThe phrase “that will never end” emphasizes the duration of the provision. Unlike earthly provisions, which are scarce, uncertain, and temporary, the provision of Paradise is endless. It will never run out, never diminish, never fail. The endlessness is one of the most precious qualities of the Paradise provision, because it addresses one of the deepest human fears: the fear of loss, of depletion, of the good things coming to an end. In Paradise, that fear is gone, because the provision, by divine guarantee, will never end.\n\nThe contrast with earthly provision is instructive. On earth, even the best provisions are temporary: wealth can be lost, health can fail, relationships can end, life itself is brief. The endlessness of the Paradise provision stands in stark contrast, offering a security that earthly provision can never give. The contrast highlights the value of the Paradise reward and underscores the wisdom of orienting one’s life toward the provision that lasts rather than toward the provisions that pass.\n\nThe word “indeed” emphasizes the reality of the identification. This is, truly, certainly, God’s provision. The emphasis underscores the certainty of the provision and the reliability of the identification. The listener is not being offered a possibility but a reality, a provision that is truly God’s and that will truly never end. The emphasis invites the listener to take the provision seriously, to value it appropriately, and to order life toward its attainment.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an assurance of the endlessness of God’s provision and a call to value the provision that lasts. It invites us to recognize that the Paradise described is God’s provision, sharing the reliability and the generosity of the Giver, and that its endlessness offers a security that no earthly provision can match. The verse challenges us to value the provision that will never end above the provisions that pass away, to orient our lives toward the attainment of the endless provision, and to trust that the One who provides it is faithful, so that the provision, like the Provider, will never fail, never diminish, and never come to an end.",
  "55": "“That is that. And the transgressors will certainly have the worst destination.” This verse marks a transition from the description of Paradise to the description of the fate of the transgressors. The phrase “that is that” closes the discussion of the righteous and their reward, indicating that the matter is settled, the destination of the righteous is fixed. Then the verse turns to the transgressors, who will certainly have the worst destination. The certainty and the contrast make the transition stark and sobering.\n\nThe significance of the transition lies in its juxtaposition of the two destinies. The righteous have the Gardens of Eternity, the open gates, the reclining, the abundance. The transgressors have the worst destination. The juxtaposition is deliberate, presenting the two outcomes side by side so that the listener can see, in the sharpest possible contrast, what is at stake in the choice between righteousness and transgression. The contrast is meant to motivate the right choice.\n\nThe phrase “that is that” conveys a sense of finality. The matter of the righteous is settled. Their destination is fixed, their reward is assured. The finality underscores the certainty of the outcome and closes the discussion with a note of settled resolution. The same finality will apply, with opposite content, to the transgressors: their destination, too, is fixed, their outcome, too, is assured. The finality of both outcomes underscores the seriousness of the present, the time of choosing, before the destinations are entered.\n\nThe word “certainly” emphasizes the sureness of the transgressors’ fate. They will certainly have the worst destination. The certainty removes any hope that transgression might, in some cases, escape consequence. The worst destination is as sure for the transgressors as the Gardens are for the righteous. The certainty is meant to warn the listener, to make clear that transgression leads, without exception, to the worst destination, and that the only escape is to cease from transgression and turn to righteousness.\n\nThe phrase “the worst destination” describes the fate of the transgressors in the most negative terms. It is not merely a bad destination but the worst, the lowest, the most terrible. The superlative underscores the severity of the fate and the completeness of its contrast with the Gardens. The worst destination is the fitting outcome of a life of transgression, the destination that the choices of the transgressors have earned and that the justice of God assigns.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a stark presentation of the contrast between the two destinies and a solemn warning about the fate of the transgressors. It invites us to recognize that the destinations are fixed, that the righteous and the transgressors face opposite outcomes, and that the worst destination awaits those who choose transgression. The verse challenges us to examine our own lives, to ensure that we are among the righteous rather than the transgressors, and to take seriously the certainty of the worst destination, allowing the warning to motivate the turning that can still, in this time of choosing, redirect our course from the worst destination toward the Gardens that await the righteous.",
  "56": "“Hell, where they will burn. What an evil place to rest!” This verse identifies the worst destination of the transgressors as Hell, where they will burn. The destination is named and its central reality described: burning. The exclamation, “what an evil place to rest,” conveys the horror of the destination through its bitter irony. Hell is a place of rest, in the sense that it is a final abode, but it is an evil place to rest, a place of torment rather than of peace. The bitter irony underscores the dreadfulness of the fate.\n\nThe significance of the identification lies in its concreteness. The worst destination is not left vague but is named as Hell and described in terms of its central experience: burning. The concreteness makes the warning vivid and inescapable. The transgressors are not headed for an abstract “worst destination” but for a specific, terrible place whose defining reality is burning. The concreteness is meant to make the warning felt, to give the listener a tangible sense of what transgression leads to.\n\nThe description of the burning as the central reality of Hell is significant. Fire, throughout the Quran, is the predominant image for the punishment of the wicked. The burning conveys intensity, pain, destruction, and the consuming nature of the consequence. The transgressors, who in this life may have enjoyed comfort and ease, will find their destination defined by an experience utterly opposed to comfort: the burning of the Fire. The reversal is complete and terrible.\n\nThe exclamation “what an evil place to rest” uses bitter irony to convey the horror. The word “rest” suggests repose, settlement, the end of a journey. Hell is, in a sense, a place of rest: it is the final abode of the transgressors, the place where their journey ends. But it is an evil place to rest, a place where the rest is not peace but torment, not comfort but suffering. The irony of evil rest underscores the dreadfulness of the fate: the transgressors will find rest, but it will be the worst possible kind, the rest of those who have arrived at a destination they would have given anything to avoid.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a concrete and sobering description of the fate of the transgressors and a warning about the reality of Hell. It invites us to recognize that the worst destination is Hell, that its central reality is burning, and that the rest it offers is evil rather than peaceful. The verse challenges us to take seriously the reality of Hell, to avoid the transgression that leads to it, and to order our lives toward the destination of peace rather than the destination of burning, knowing that the choice between the two is the most important choice we will ever make, and that the destinations, once entered, are as final and as real as the verse so vividly and so terribly portrays.",
  "57": "“Let them then taste this: boiling water and oozing pus.” This verse describes specific elements of the punishment of Hell, directed at the transgressors. Let them taste, the verse declares, the boiling water and the oozing pus. The description is deliberately repulsive, presenting the punishment in terms that engage the senses with images of disgust and pain. The tasting is not a metaphor but a reality that the transgressors will experience.\n\nThe significance of the description lies in its sensory vividness and its deliberate repulsiveness. The Quran does not describe Hell in vague or abstract terms but in concrete, sensory images that convey the reality and the dreadfulness of the punishment. The boiling water and the oozing pus are images chosen for their capacity to evoke both pain and disgust, communicating the severity of the punishment through sensations that the listener can readily imagine.\n\nThe boiling water addresses the experience of burning through a liquid medium. The transgressors, in the Fire, will be given boiling water to drink, water that scalds as it is consumed, that brings pain rather than relief. The image is horrifying because it inverts the normal function of water: water, which normally refreshes and sustains, becomes, in Hell, a means of torment. The inversion underscores the perversion of the punishment, where even the most basic means of relief become sources of suffering.\n\nThe oozing pus is an image of extreme repulsiveness, presenting the punishment in terms that evoke disgust as well as pain. The pus, the discharge of infection, is among the most repulsive of bodily substances, and its presence as an element of the punishment conveys the degradation and the horror of the Hellish experience. The image is meant to repel, to make the listener feel the dreadfulness of the destination, and to motivate, through the very repulsiveness of the image, the avoidance of the conduct that leads to it.\n\nThe word “taste” is significant. The punishment is not merely observed but tasted, experienced personally and directly. The tasting conveys the immediacy and the intimacy of the suffering: the transgressors will not merely see the punishment from a distance but will experience it in their own bodies, tasting its bitterness and its pain. The tasting is the fulfillment of the warning, the moment when the abstract threat becomes concrete experience.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a vivid and repulsive description of the punishment of Hell, meant to convey its dreadfulness through sensory images. It invites us to recognize the severity of the punishment, the reality of the suffering that awaits the transgressors, and the importance of avoiding the conduct that leads to it. The verse challenges us to take seriously the images of Hell, however repulsive, to allow their dreadfulness to motivate our avoidance of transgression, and to order our lives toward the destination of the righteous, whose refreshment is cool water and delicious fruit, rather than toward the destination of the transgressors, whose tasting is boiling water and oozing pus, the terrible inversion of every good gift into a source of suffering.",
  "58": "“And other torments of the same sort!” This verse extends the description of the punishment of Hell beyond the specific elements just named. In addition to the boiling water and the oozing pus, there are other torments of the same sort. The extension conveys the variety and the comprehensiveness of the punishment, suggesting that the specific elements named are representative rather than exhaustive, and that the transgressors will face a range of torments, all terrible, all of the same dreadful character.\n\nThe significance of the extension lies in its communication of the comprehensiveness of the punishment. The specific elements named, the boiling water and the pus, are terrible enough, but they are not the whole of the punishment. There are other torments, many others, of the same sort. The comprehensiveness underscores the severity of the fate and the completeness of the consequence. The transgressors will not face a single, uniform punishment but a variety of torments, each terrible, together forming a comprehensive experience of suffering.\n\nThe phrase “of the same sort” indicates the character of the additional torments. They are not milder or different in kind but of the same sort as those named, equally terrible, equally painful, equally repulsive. The consistency of the character means that the punishment, in all its variety, maintains the same level of severity. There is no relief, no variation toward the milder, no respite within the variety. Every torment is of the same dreadful sort, contributing to a comprehensive experience of suffering from which there is no escape.\n\nThe extension also serves to convey the inexhaustibility of the punishment. The specific elements named could be counted, but the “other torments” cannot, suggesting that the punishment exceeds any enumeration. The inexhaustibility reflects the boundlessness of the divine justice in response to the boundlessness of the transgression. The transgressors, who sinned without measure, will be punished without measure, facing an inexhaustible variety of torments that correspond to the inexhaustible variety of their sins.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an extension of the description of Hell’s punishment and a communication of its comprehensiveness and inexhaustibility. It invites us to recognize that the punishment of Hell is not limited to the specific elements named but encompasses a variety of torments, all terrible, all of the same dreadful character, and that the comprehensiveness of the punishment corresponds to the seriousness of the transgression it addresses. The verse challenges us to take seriously the boundlessness of the punishment, to avoid the transgression that leads to it, and to order our lives toward the destination of peace, whose delights are also comprehensive and inexhaustible, but in the opposite direction, offering an eternity of varied and unending good rather than the eternity of varied and unending torment that awaits the transgressors.",
  "59": "“Here is a crowd of followers being thrown in with us. They are not welcome, for they too will burn in the Fire.” This verse records the words of the misleaders in Hell as they see their followers being thrown in with them. The misleaders, who in this life led others astray, now see their followers arriving in the same dreadful destination. The arrival prompts a cruel response: the misleaders declare that the followers are not welcome, acknowledging that they too will burn. The scene reveals the collapse of the bonds of misguidance and the cruelty that characterized the relationships built on error.\n\nThe significance of the scene lies in its revelation of the true nature of the relationships built on misguidance. In this life, the misleaders and their followers may have appeared to be allies, partners in a shared direction. But in Hell, the appearance gives way to the reality: the misleaders do not welcome their followers but reject them, declaring them unwelcome. The rejection reveals that the bonds of misguidance were never genuine, that the alliance was based on mutual use rather than on mutual care, and that the crisis of Hell exposes the hollowness of every relationship not built on truth.\n\nThe phrase “a crowd of followers being thrown in with us” describes the arrival of the followers in Hell. The followers are thrown in, suggesting force and compulsion, the inevitable arrival of those whose choices have earned this destination. The crowd suggests the number of the followers, the many who were misled and who now share the fate of those who misled them. The arrival completes the solidarity of misguidance: those who walked together in error now arrive together in consequence.\n\nThe declaration “they are not welcome” reveals the cruelty and the selfishness of the misleaders. Having led the followers into Hell, the misleaders now reject them, refusing even the comfort of companionship in suffering. The rejection is a final act of betrayal: the misleaders, who gained followers in this life, disown them in the next. The cruelty exposes the true character of the misleaders, who never cared for their followers but only used them, and who, in the crisis, abandon them.\n\nThe acknowledgment “they too will burn in the Fire” confirms the shared fate. The misleaders recognize that the followers, like themselves, will burn. The recognition offers no comfort but only the cold acknowledgment of a shared doom. The burning that the misleaders endure will also be endured by those they misled. The shared burning is the final outcome of the shared misguidance, the consequence that unites those whom error had joined.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering revelation of the collapse of the bonds of misguidance and of the cruelty that underlies them. It invites us to recognize that relationships built on error are hollow, that the misleaders will abandon their followers in the crisis, and that the solidarity of misguidance becomes, in Hell, a solidarity of suffering without comfort. The verse challenges us to examine the relationships and the influences in our lives, to ensure that they are built on truth rather than on error, and to avoid both the role of the misleader and the role of the misled, knowing that the bonds of misguidance, however strong they appear in this life, will collapse in the next, leaving only the shared burning and the bitter recognition that the alliance of error was, from the beginning, a partnership in ruin.",
  "60": "“The followers will respond, No! You are not welcome! You brought this upon us. What an evil place for settlement!” This verse records the response of the followers to the rejection of the misleaders. The followers turn the rejection back: no, you are not welcome. They blame the misleaders for bringing the punishment upon them. And they lament the evil of the place. The response completes the collapse of the alliance of misguidance, as each party rejects and blames the other.\n\nThe significance of the response lies in its revelation of the mutual recrimination that characterizes the relationships of Hell. The misleaders rejected the followers; the followers reject the misleaders. Each party declares the other unwelcome. The mutual rejection exposes the complete breakdown of every bond that had united them. The alliance that seemed so strong in this life dissolves, in Hell, into mutual hostility, with each side blaming the other for the shared doom.\n\nThe phrase “you brought this upon us” reveals the followers’ attempt to shift the blame. They attribute their fate to the misleaders, claiming that the misleaders are responsible for the punishment that has overtaken them. The claim contains a partial truth: the misleaders did lead the followers astray and bear responsibility for their misleading. But the claim is also an evasion: the followers chose to follow, consented to the misleading, and bear their own responsibility for their fate. The attempt to shift the blame entirely onto the misleaders is unjust, however understandable.\n\nThe lament “what an evil place for settlement” echoes the earlier exclamation about Hell as an evil place to rest. The followers, like the misleaders, recognize the dreadfulness of their destination. The recognition adds nothing to their relief, for there is no relief, but it completes the picture of their suffering: not only do they endure the torments of Hell, they also recognize, with full and terrible clarity, the evil of the place where they have settled. The recognition itself is part of the torment, the clear sight of what they have brought upon themselves.\n\nThe scene as a whole, the mutual rejection, the blame-shifting, the lament, reveals the complete failure of the alliance of misguidance to provide any comfort or support in the crisis. The misleaders and the followers, who supported each other in error, now attack each other in consequence. The bonds that seemed to unite them are revealed as bonds of mutual destruction, and the shared doom becomes a shared misery, intensified by the mutual hostility that replaces the false solidarity of their earthly alliance.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a sobering picture of the mutual recrimination of Hell and a warning about the nature of alliances built on misguidance. It invites us to recognize that such alliances provide no comfort in the crisis, that they dissolve into mutual blame, and that neither the misleaders nor the misled find any support in the other when the consequence arrives. The verse challenges us to avoid both misleading and being misled, to build our relationships on truth rather than on error, and to take personal responsibility for our choices, knowing that the blame-shifting of Hell will accomplish nothing, and that the only safety lies in choosing, in this life, the path of truth that leads to the destination of peace rather than the path of error that leads to the evil place of settlement where mutual recrimination replaces every bond and where blame, however fiercely assigned, changes nothing.",
  "61": "“Our Lord! Whoever brought this upon us, double their punishment in the Fire.” This verse records the further words of the followers in Hell. Having blamed the misleaders for their fate, the followers now call on God to double the punishment of those who brought the doom upon them. The call is for increased punishment for the misleaders, a recognition that those who lead others astray bear a heavier guilt than those who are led.\n\nThe significance of the call lies in its recognition of the greater guilt of the misleaders. The followers, even as they share the punishment, recognize that those who led them into it bear a greater responsibility. The call for doubled punishment reflects the principle, found throughout the Quran, that the one who misleads others carries a heavier burden than the one who is misled. The misleaders not only went astray themselves but caused others to go astray, and the double harm earns, in the view of the followers, a double punishment.\n\nThe phrase “whoever brought this upon us” identifies the target of the call. The followers focus on those whose misleading led to their current fate. The identification is specific: the punishment is called for not on the followers themselves, who also bear responsibility, but on the misleaders, whose role the followers emphasize. The focus on the misleaders reflects the followers’ attempt to deflect blame from themselves and to place it, with increased severity, on those they hold responsible.\n\nThe call for the punishment to be doubled reveals the followers’ sense of the proportion of guilt. The misleaders, in the followers’ view, deserve more punishment because their wrongdoing was greater. The doubling is not an arbitrary increase but a recognition of the greater harm caused by misleading. The one who ruins only himself suffers one measure; the one who ruins himself and others suffers more. The principle of proportionate punishment, reflected in the followers’ call, corresponds to the justice of God, who assigns to each a punishment fitted to their deeds.\n\nThe address “Our Lord!” is significant. Even in Hell, the followers address God as Lord, acknowledging His authority and appealing to His justice. The acknowledgment reveals that the truth about God, denied or ignored in this life, is recognized in the next. The followers, who may have followed the misleaders away from God, now acknowledge Him as Lord and appeal to Him for justice against those who misled them. The acknowledgment comes too late to change their fate, but it confirms the reality of God’s lordship that the followers had failed to honor in the time of choosing.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a revelation of the greater guilt of those who mislead others and a warning about the responsibility that comes with influence. It invites us to recognize that misleading others carries a heavier consequence than going astray alone, that the harm we cause to others through our influence adds to our own account, and that the justice of God takes full account of the role each person played in the misguidance of others. The verse challenges us to use our influence for good rather than for ill, to lead others toward truth rather than away from it, and to recognize that the responsibility of influence is serious, knowing that the one who misleads others earns, in addition to the punishment of his own error, the weight of the harm he has caused to those he led astray, and that the justice of God will assign, with perfect fairness, to each the punishment that their role and their deeds deserve.",
  "62": "“But why do we not see those we considered to be lowly?” This verse records the words of the tyrants in Hell, asking one another why they do not see the people they had considered lowly in the world. The tyrants, who had looked down on the believers, now search for them in Hell and do not find them. The question reveals the tyrants’ expectation that the believers, whom they despised, would share their fate, and their surprise, even in their torment, at not finding them there.\n\nThe significance of the question lies in its revelation of the tyrants’ worldview and its exposure. The tyrants had considered the believers lowly, beneath them in status, wealth, or power. They had assumed that their own worldly superiority corresponded to a superior destiny, that the people they despised would, in the end, share their fate or worse. The question, asked from within Hell, reveals the collapse of this assumption: the believers are not there. The despised are absent from the punishment; the despisers are present in it. The reversal exposes the hollowness of the tyrants’ worldly judgments.\n\nThe question also reveals the tyrants’ preoccupation, even in their torment, with the believers. They cannot let go of the comparison that shaped their earthly lives. Even in Hell, they are looking for the people they despised, perhaps hoping to find them sharing the punishment, which would offer some bitter comfort. The preoccupation reveals the depth of the worldly mindset that still grips them, the persistent habit of measuring themselves against others, even when the measuring is meaningless and the comparison can bring no relief.\n\nThe absence of the believers from Hell is the unspoken answer to the question. The tyrants do not see the lowly because the lowly are not there. The believers, despised on earth, are honored in Paradise, far from the Fire that consumes the tyrants. The absence is itself a commentary on the reversal that the Day of Judgment brings: the lowly are exalted, the mighty are humbled, and the judgments of the world are overturned by the verdict of God.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a revelation of the reversal that the Day of Judgment brings and a warning about the hollowness of worldly judgments of status. It invites us to recognize that the people the world despises may be the ones God honors, that worldly lowliness does not correspond to eternal lowliness, and that the tyrants’ surprise at not finding the believers in Hell exposes the error of their earthly assumptions. The verse challenges us to examine our own judgments of others, to avoid the contempt that the tyrants showed, and to recognize that the measure of worth is not worldly status but faith and righteousness, which may belong as easily to the lowly as to the great, and which determine, in the end, who is honored and who is punished.",
  "63": "“Were we wrong in mocking them in the world? Or do our eyes just fail to see them in the Fire?” This verse records the continuing words of the tyrants in Hell. They pose two possibilities to explain the absence of the believers. Either they were wrong to mock them in the world, implying that the believers’ absence proves the believers were right and the mocking was mistaken. Or their eyes fail to see them in the Fire, implying that the believers are present but invisible. The two possibilities reveal the tyrants’ confusion and their reluctance to accept the implications of the believers’ absence.\n\nThe significance of the verse lies in its exposure of the tyrants’ self-questioning. In the world, the tyrants mocked the believers with confidence, certain of their own superiority. In Hell, that confidence is gone, replaced by a questioning that exposes the doubt the mocking had always masked. The question “were we wrong” reveals, at last, a crack in the tyrants’ certainty, a recognition that their earthly conduct may have been mistaken. The recognition, however, comes too late to help them, for they are already in the Fire, and the wrongness of their mocking is now an established fact rather than a correctable error.\n\nThe first possibility, “were we wrong in mocking them,” contains an implicit acknowledgment. If the believers are not in Hell, then the mocking was wrong, and the believers, whom the tyrants despised, were in fact on the right path. The acknowledgment is devastating, because it means that the tyrants not only suffer the Fire but also suffer the recognition that they brought it on themselves through their rejection of the truth the believers represented. The mocking, which seemed like scornful superiority, was actually folly, and the believers, whom the tyrants ridiculed, were the wise ones all along.\n\nThe second possibility, “do our eyes just fail to see them,” reveals the tyrants’ reluctance to accept the implications of the believers’ absence. Rather than concluding that the believers are not in Hell because they are in Paradise, the tyrants consider the possibility that the believers are present but unseen. The reluctance reflects the difficulty of accepting the reversal, the challenge to the tyrants’ worldview that the believers’ absence represents. The clinging to the possibility of the believers’ presence, even invisibly, shows how hard it is for the tyrants to let go of their earthly assumptions, even when those assumptions have been thoroughly overturned.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a revelation of the tyrants’ too-late self-questioning and a warning about the folly of mocking the faithful. It invites us to recognize that the mocking of the believers is a serious wrong, that those who mock may discover, too late, the error of their mockery, and that the confidence that sustains contempt in this life may collapse into doubt in the next. The verse challenges us to examine our own attitudes toward the faithful, to avoid the mocking that the tyrants practiced, and to recognize, before it is too late, that the believers whom the world despises may be the ones whom God honors, and that the mocking of them is not a sign of superiority but of the very folly that leads, as it led the tyrants, to the Fire where the recognition of error comes too late to help.",
  "64": "“This dispute between the residents of the Fire will certainly come to pass.” This verse states the certainty of the disputes that have just been described among the residents of Hell. The mutual accusations, the blame-shifting, the questions about the missing believers, all of this dispute will certainly take place. The certainty is marked by the emphatic wording. The verse assures the listener that the scenes of Hellish dispute, however vivid and terrible, are not imaginative speculation but certain realities that will unfold.\n\nThe significance of the certainty lies in its function as a warning. By affirming that the disputes will certainly come to pass, the verse removes any possibility of dismissing the scenes as mere rhetoric. The disputes are real, certain, guaranteed. The affirmation is meant to sober the listener, to make clear that the Hell being described is not a figure of speech but a reality, and that the disputes among its residents are among the features of that reality. The certainty underscores the seriousness of the warning and the importance of heeding it before it is too late.\n\nThe word “dispute” captures the nature of the interactions among the residents of Hell. They do not comfort one another or support one another but dispute, arguing, accusing, blaming. The dispute is itself part of the torment: the residents of Hell cannot even find in one another any solace, because their interactions are characterized by conflict rather than by care. The dispute compounds the suffering, adding relational misery to physical torment, and ensuring that the residents of Hell experience not only the pain of the Fire but also the pain of mutual hostility.\n\nThe phrase “the residents of the Fire” identifies the participants in the dispute. They are the residents, the inhabitants, those whose dwelling is the Fire. The identification is permanent: they are not visitors but residents, settled in their dreadful home. The permanence of the residence underscores the finality of the fate and the futility of the dispute, which can change nothing about the residence but only adds bitterness to an already bitter existence.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an assurance of the certainty of the Hellish realities and a warning about the nature of existence in the Fire. It invites us to recognize that the scenes of dispute are certain, that the residents of Hell will turn on one another in mutual accusation rather than finding comfort in company, and that the relational misery of Hell compounds its physical torment. The verse challenges us to take the warning seriously, to avoid the conduct that leads to the Fire, and to order our lives toward the destination of peace, where the residents find in one another not dispute but harmony, not accusation but love, and where the company of the blessed is a source of eternal joy rather than of the certain and bitter dispute that this verse so solemnly affirms.",
  "65": "“I am only a warner. And there is no god worthy of worship except Allah, the One, the Supreme.” This verse records the message the Prophet is told to declare. He is, first, only a warner, a servant tasked with alerting humanity to the truth and its consequences. And the core of the truth he declares is the oneness of God: there is no god worthy of worship except Allah, the One, the Supreme. The declaration combines the Prophet’s role with the central message, presenting both in a single, powerful statement.\n\nThe significance of the declaration lies in its combination of the Prophet’s humility and the message’s grandeur. The Prophet is “only a warner,” a phrase that emphasizes his limited role and his dependence on God. He is not a controller of hearts or a guarantor of outcomes but a warner, one who alerts. The humility of the role stands in sharp contrast with the grandeur of the message he declares: the oneness of God, the most important truth a human being can know. The contrast reveals the nature of prophethood: the messenger is humble, but the message is supreme.\n\nThe phrase “there is no god worthy of worship except Allah” is the central declaration of Islam, the testimony of divine oneness. The declaration affirms that God alone is worthy of worship and denies the divinity of everything else. The declaration is the foundation of all genuine faith, the truth from which every other truth flows. By including this declaration in the Prophet’s message, the verse places the oneness of God at the heart of what the Prophet came to convey.\n\nThe description of God as “the One, the Supreme” reinforces the declaration. God is One, unique, without partner or rival. And He is Supreme, exalted above all, possessing the highest authority. The two attributes together establish the absoluteness of God’s oneness and the comprehensiveness of His supremacy. The One is supreme, and the Supreme is one. The combination leaves no room for any competing deity or any shared authority.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a clear statement of the Prophet’s role and message and a call to receive both. It invites us to recognize the Prophet as a warner, humble and dependent, and to receive his message, the declaration of God’s oneness, with the seriousness it deserves. The verse challenges us to embrace the truth that there is no god worthy of worship except Allah, the One, the Supreme, to direct all our worship to Him alone, and to honor the Prophet as the bearer of this supreme message, knowing that the warner’s role, however humble, carries the most important truth a human being can hear, and that the receiving of it is the gateway to the life of faith that the warning is meant to awaken.",
  "66": "“He is the Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything in between, the Almighty, Most Forgiving.” This verse expands on the declaration of God’s oneness by describing His lordship and His attributes. He is the Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything in between, the sovereign of the entire cosmos. And He is the Almighty, Most Forgiving, possessing both unlimited power and boundless mercy. The description grounds the declaration of oneness in the reality of who God is.\n\nThe significance of the description lies in its comprehensive presentation of God’s nature. The lordship over the heavens and the earth establishes God’s sovereignty over all creation. The might establishes His power to accomplish His will. The forgiveness establishes His mercy toward His servants. The combination presents a God who is both supreme and compassionate, both powerful and forgiving, a complete picture that invites both reverence and hope.\n\nThe phrase “Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything in between” establishes the scope of God’s lordship. Everything that exists, from the highest heaven to the lowest depth of the earth, falls under His sovereignty. There is nothing outside His lordship, no realm, no creature, no force, that is not under His authority. The comprehensiveness of the lordship grounds the declaration of oneness: because God is Lord of all, He alone deserves worship.\n\nThe description “the Almighty” establishes God’s power. He is mighty, possessing the strength to do whatever He wills, to enforce His decrees, to accomplish His purposes. The might is not abstract but active: God exercises His power in the world, sustaining creation, guiding history, and administering justice. The might inspires reverence and assures the believer that God is able to fulfill every promise and to carry out every warning.\n\nThe description “Most Forgiving” establishes God’s mercy. He is forgiving, ready to pardon the sins of those who turn to Him, to cover their shortcomings, to restore them to His favor. The forgiveness balances the might, presenting a God who is not only powerful but also compassionate. The balance is precious: a God of power without forgiveness would be terrifying; a God of forgiveness without power would be helpless. The God of the Quran is both, mighty enough to be obeyed and forgiving enough to be approached.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a comprehensive presentation of God’s nature and a call to respond to Him with both reverence and hope. It invites us to recognize God as the Lord of all, the Almighty, the Most Forgiving, and to approach Him with the awe that His power demands and the confidence that His forgiveness inspires. The verse challenges us to order our lives under the lordship of the God who rules the heavens and the earth, to revere His might, to seek His forgiveness, and to trust that the same God who possesses all power also possesses all mercy, and that His forgiveness is as real as His might, available to all who turn to Him in sincere repentance and faith.",
  "67": "“This Quran is momentous news.” This verse describes the Quran as momentous news. The word “momentous” carries the sense of great, significant, weighty. The Quran is not trivial or ordinary but momentous, carrying a significance that demands attention. The description elevates the Quran above every other communication and presents it as something that no one can afford to ignore.\n\nThe significance of the description lies in its identification of the Quran’s importance. The Quran is news, a communication, a message. And it is momentous news, a message of supreme importance. The description challenges any tendency to treat the Quran casually, as one book among many, or to regard its message as optional. The momentousness of the news means that the Quran demands a response, that its message cannot be received with indifference, and that the stakes of accepting or rejecting it are of the highest order.\n\nThe word “news” is significant. The Quran brings news, information about realities that human beings could not know on their own: the nature of God, the purpose of creation, the reality of the Hereafter, the stories of the prophets, the principles of divine justice. The news is not human speculation but divine communication, the Creator informing His creatures about the truths that matter most. The news is momentous because the realities it reports are momentous: the eternal destiny of every soul, the will of God for human life, the consequences of faith and unbelief.\n\nThe momentousness of the news also implies urgency. Momentous news demands to be heard, considered, and acted upon promptly. It cannot be set aside for later attention or treated as a matter of leisurely interest. The Quran, as momentous news, requires immediate engagement, serious consideration, and appropriate response. The urgency is part of the message: the realities the Quran reports are pressing, and the time for response is now, not later.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to recognize the momentousness of the Quran and to respond to it with the seriousness it deserves. It invites us to approach the Quran not as ordinary reading but as momentous news, to give it our full attention, to consider its message carefully, and to respond to it appropriately. The verse challenges us to take the Quran seriously, to recognize that its news is the most important news we will ever hear, and to ensure that our response to it reflects its momentousness, knowing that the realities it reports, and the response it requires, are of eternal significance, and that the news, however received, is too momentous to be ignored.",
  "68": "“From which you pagans are turning away.” This verse completes the description of the Quran by noting the response of the pagans: they are turning away from it. Despite the momentousness of the news the Quran brings, the pagans turn away, refusing to engage with it, to consider it, to respond to it. The turning away is the tragedy that the verse exposes: momentous news is offered, and the recipients turn from it.\n\nThe significance of the turning away lies in its revelation of the pagans’ response to the truth. The Quran is momentous news, demanding attention and response. The pagans, instead of attending and responding, turn away. The turning away is a choice, a deliberate refusal to engage with what has been offered. The choice exposes the pagans’ priorities: they prefer their existing ways, their idols, their traditions, to the momentous news that challenges them. The turning away is not the result of the news being unimportant but of the pagans being unwilling.\n\nThe phrase “turning away” suggests a physical as well as a spiritual gesture. The pagans turn their faces, their attention, their interest away from the Quran. The turning is active, a redirecting of their orientation away from the truth that has been presented. The image conveys the deliberateness of the refusal: the pagans are not merely neglecting the Quran but actively turning from it, choosing to face another direction, to attend to other things, to give their interest to whatever is not the momentous news the Quran brings.\n\nThe contrast between the momentousness of the Quran and the pagans’ turning away is the heart of the tragedy. The more important the news, the greater the tragedy of turning from it. If the Quran were trivial, turning away would matter little. But because the Quran is momentous, the turning away is catastrophic. The pagans are not turning from a minor matter but from the most important news they will ever hear, and the turning away will have eternal consequences that match the importance of what is being refused.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an exposure of the tragedy of turning away from the truth and a warning about the seriousness of such refusal. It invites us to recognize the catastrophe of turning from the momentous news of the Quran, to examine our own orientation toward it, and to ensure that we are not, like the pagans, turning away from the most important communication we will ever receive. The verse challenges us to turn toward the Quran, to give it our attention and our response, and to recognize that the turning away of the pagans is a warning to all who are tempted to face another direction, knowing that the momentousness of the news makes the turning from it the most consequential refusal a human being can make.",
  "69": "“I had no knowledge of the highest assembly in heaven when they differed concerning Adam.” This verse records what the Prophet is told to say about his own lack of prior knowledge of the heavenly matters. He had no knowledge of the highest assembly, the gathering of the angels, when they differed concerning Adam. The statement establishes the Prophet’s human limitation: he was not present at the heavenly assembly, he had no independent knowledge of its discussions, and the information he conveys about it comes solely from revelation.\n\nThe significance of the statement lies in its defense of the Prophet’s truthfulness and the divine origin of his message. The Prophet conveys information about heavenly matters, the assembly of the angels, the discussion concerning Adam, that he could not have known through ordinary means. By stating that he had no prior knowledge of these matters, the verse establishes that the information comes from God, not from the Prophet’s own invention. The establishment supports the authenticity of the revelation and the truthfulness of the messenger.\n\nThe phrase “the highest assembly” refers to the gathering of the angels in the presence of God. The assembly is the highest, the most exalted, the most authoritative gathering in the cosmos. The Prophet’s lack of knowledge of this assembly, and of what transpired in it, underscores the gap between the human and the heavenly. The Prophet, though a messenger, was not privy to the heavenly councils by his own access; what he knew of them came through revelation alone.\n\nThe mention of the angels differing “concerning Adam” introduces the story that the following verses will tell: the creation of Adam, the command to the angels to prostrate, and the refusal of Iblîs. The differing may refer to the discussions among the angels about the creation of a being who would cause corruption and shed blood, discussions referenced elsewhere in the Quran. The Prophet’s knowledge of these discussions, conveyed through revelation, demonstrates the divine source of his information, for he could not have known, by any human means, what was said in the highest assembly.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a defense of the Prophet’s truthfulness and a reminder that his knowledge of heavenly matters came through revelation. It invites us to recognize that the Prophet’s information about the unseen, including the heavenly assembly and the story of Adam, comes from God, not from human invention, and that the recognition supports the authenticity of the message. The verse challenges us to receive the Prophet’s accounts of the unseen as divinely revealed truth, to trust the messenger who conveyed what he could not have known on his own, and to recognize, in the gap between the Prophet’s human limitation and the heavenly content of his message, the evidence that the message is from God, communicated through a faithful servant who conveyed, without addition or alteration, what was revealed to him.",
  "70": "“What is revealed to me is that I am only sent with a clear warning.” This verse states the content of the revelation given to the Prophet. What is revealed to him is that he is only sent with a clear warning. The statement defines the Prophet’s mission with precision: he is a warner, and his warning is clear. The definition establishes both the scope and the clarity of the prophetic task.\n\nThe significance of the statement lies in its clear definition of the Prophet’s mission. He is “only sent with a clear warning.” The word “only” limits the mission: the Prophet is not sent to compel, to force, or to guarantee outcomes, but to warn. The limitation frees the Prophet from the impossible burden of producing belief and confines his responsibility to the faithful delivery of the warning. The clarity of the definition offers both guidance and comfort: guidance about what the Prophet is to do, and comfort in knowing that his duty is bounded.\n\nThe phrase “clear warning” emphasizes the nature of the Prophet’s message. The warning is not obscure, ambiguous, or confusing but clear, plain, understandable. The clarity is a mercy, because it ensures that the warning can be understood by all who hear it, leaving no one with the excuse of confusion or ignorance. The clarity also reflects the nature of God’s communication: God does not muddle His message but conveys it with a clarity that enables genuine response.\n\nThe word “warning” identifies the core of the Prophet’s message. He warns, alerting humanity to the realities that demand attention: the oneness of God, the coming judgment, the consequences of unbelief, the reward of faith. The warning is an act of mercy, because it alerts people to dangers they might otherwise ignore and gives them the opportunity to turn before it is too late. A warner who is faithful to his task performs a deeply compassionate service, even when the warning is unwelcome.\n\nThe connection between this verse and the preceding ones is significant. The Prophet has been described as one who had no independent knowledge of the heavenly assembly, whose information about the unseen comes solely through revelation. Now the content of the revelation is stated: he is sent with a clear warning. The two together present the Prophet as a faithful conduit: receiving from God what he could not know on his own, and conveying it clearly as a warning to humanity. The combination establishes the authenticity of the message and the faithfulness of the messenger.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a clear definition of the Prophet’s mission and a call to receive his warning. It invites us to recognize the Prophet as a warner, sent with a clear message, and to receive the warning with the seriousness that its clarity and its importance demand. The verse challenges us to attend to the clear warning, to allow it to alert us to the realities we might otherwise ignore, and to respond to it with faith and action, knowing that the warning is an act of mercy, meant not to frighten but to save, and that the receiving of it, with appropriate response, is the path to the safety that the warner was sent to offer.",
  "71": "“˹Remember, O Prophet˺ when your Lord said to the angels, “I am going to create a human being from clay.”” records the announcement, and the commentators note the two parts of the announcement. The verse records the announcement: or \"ith qala rabbuka lil-mala'ikati innee khaliqun basharan min teen,\" and the commentators note the announcement, for the Lord declared the creation, and the announcement is the revelation, the beginning of the story, and the verse is the record of the announcement, the revelation that the following verse commands, and the commentators note the significance, for the creation is the announcement, and the verse is the record of the announcement, the revelation of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the announcement is the revelation and the creation is the clay, and the verse is the record of the announcement, the revelation that the following verse commands.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the announcement, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the creation of the human is the clay, and the one who remembers is the one whose humility the Lord values, and the believer who knows the origin is the believer whose humility the Lord accepts, and the announcement of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the announcement is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humility of the origin: the human is the clay, and the believer who knows the origin is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the announcement is the teaching that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the announcement, and the teaching of the humility that the origin requires.",
  "72": "“So when I have fashioned him and had a spirit of My Own ˹creation˺ breathed into him, fall down in prostration to him.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: or \"fa-itha sawwaytuhu wa nafakhtu feehi min roohee faqa'oo lahu sajideen,\" and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded the prostration, and the command is the test, the honour of the human, and the verse is the record of the command, the test that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the prostration is the test, and the verse is the record of the command, the test of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the test and the prostration is the honour, and the verse is the record of the command, the test that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the prostration is the obedience, and the one who obeys is the one whose obedience the Lord values, and the believer who bows to the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the command of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the obedience of the prostration: the command was the test, and the believer who obeys is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the obedience that the test requires.",
  "73": "“So the angels prostrated all together—” records the prostration, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the prostration: or \"fa-sajada al-mala'ikatu kulluhum ajma'oon,\" and the commentators note the prostration, for the angels prostrated, and the prostration is the obedience, the submission of the angels, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the obedience that the following verse contrasts, and the commentators note the significance, for the prostration is the obedience, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the obedience of the angels. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the prostration is the obedience and the submission is the angels, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the obedience that the following verse contrasts.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prostration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the obedience of the angels is the model, and the one who obeys is the one whose obedience the Lord values, and the believer who bows to the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the prostration of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the prostration is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the obedience of the angels: the angels prostrated, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the prostration is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the prostration, and the teaching of the obedience that the command requires.",
  "74": "“but not Iblîs, who acted arrogantly, becoming unfaithful.”” records the refusal, and the commentators note the two parts of the refusal. The verse records the refusal: or \"illa Ibleesa istakbara wa kana mina al-kafireen,\" and the commentators note the refusal, for Iblis refused and became the unfaithful, and the refusal is the arrogance, the pride of the enemy, and the verse is the record of the refusal, the arrogance that the following verse examines, and the commentators note the significance, for the refusal is the arrogance, and the verse is the record of the refusal, the arrogance of Iblis. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the refusal is the arrogance and the pride is the unfaithful, and the verse is the record of the refusal, the arrogance that the following verse examines.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the refusal, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the refusal of the command is the arrogance, and the one who refuses is the one whose arrogance the Lord condemns, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the refusal of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the refusal is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the arrogance: Iblis refused and was cast out, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the refusal is the teaching that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the refusal, and the teaching of the humility that the arrogance requires.",
  "75": "“Allah asked, “O Iblîs! What prevented you from prostrating to what I created with My Own Hands? Did you ˹just˺ become proud? Or have you always been arrogant?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two parts of the question. The verse records the question: or \"qala ya Ibleesu ma mana'aka an tasjuda lima khalaqtu bi-yadayya astakbarta am kunta mina al-'aleen,\" and the commentators note the question, for the Lord asked Iblis, and the question is the examination, the exposure of the arrogance, and the verse is the record of the question, the examination that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the examination, and the verse is the record of the question, the examination of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the examination and the exposure is the arrogance, and the verse is the record of the question, the examination that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the examination of the Lord is the justice, and the one who is asked is the one whose answer the Lord weighs, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the question of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the examination of the Lord: Iblis was asked and exposed, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the question is the teaching that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the humility that the examination requires.",
  "76": "“He replied, “I am better than he is: You created me from fire and him from clay.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qala ana khayrun minhu khalaqtanee min narin wa khalaqtahu min teen,\" and the commentators note the reply, for Iblis compared the origins, and the reply is the arrogance, the claim of the superiority, and the verse is the record of the reply, the arrogance that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the comparison is the arrogance, and the verse is the record of the reply, the arrogance of Iblis. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the arrogance and the comparison is the error, and the verse is the record of the reply, the arrogance that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the comparison of the origins is the error, and the one who compares is the one whose error the Lord condemns, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the reply of Iblis is the teaching, the declaration that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the error of the comparison: Iblis compared and was cast out, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the humility that the comparison requires.",
  "77": "“Allah commanded, “Then get out of Paradise, for you are truly cursed.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: or \"qala fakhruj minha fa-innaka rajeem,\" and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded the exit, and the command is the judgment, the expulsion of the enemy, and the verse is the record of the command, the judgment that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the expulsion is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the command, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the judgment and the expulsion is the curse, and the verse is the record of the command, the judgment that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the expulsion of the arrogant is the judgment, and the one who is expelled is the one whose arrogance the Lord condemns, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the command of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: Iblis was expelled, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the humility that the judgment requires.",
  "78": "“And surely upon you is My condemnation until the Day of Judgment.”” completes the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the command: or \"wa inna 'alayka la'natee ila yawmi ad-deen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord declared the curse, and the completion is the judgment, the duration of the condemnation, and the verse is the record of the completion, the judgment that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the curse is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the completion, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the judgment and the curse is the duration, and the verse is the record of the completion, the judgment that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the curse of the arrogant is the judgment, and the one who is cursed is the one whose arrogance the Lord condemns, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the curse is upon Iblis, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the humility that the judgment requires.",
  "79": "“Satan appealed, “My Lord! Then delay my end until the Day of their resurrection.”” records the appeal, and the commentators note the two parts of the appeal. The verse records the appeal: or \"qala rabbi fa-anzirnee ila yawmi yub'athoon,\" and the commentators note the appeal, for Iblis asked the delay, and the appeal is the request, the seeking of the time, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the request that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the appeal is the request, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the request of Iblis. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the appeal is the request and the delay is the time, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the request that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the appeal, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own awareness. The believer is taught that the enemy of the believer is the one who requested the time, and the one who knows is the one whose awareness the Lord values, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose awareness the Lord accepts, and the appeal of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the awareness is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the appeal is the believer whose awareness the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the awareness of the enemy: Iblis requested the time, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose awareness the Lord values, and the appeal is the teaching that the awareness is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the appeal, and the teaching of the awareness that the enemy requires.",
  "80": "“Allah said, “You will be delayed”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the answer: or \"qala fa-innaka mina al-munzareen,\" and the commentators note the answer, for the Lord granted the delay, and the answer is the permission, the allowance of the time, and the verse is the record of the answer, the permission that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the delay is the permission, and the verse is the record of the answer, the permission of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the answer is the permission and the delay is the time, and the verse is the record of the answer, the permission that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the answer, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own awareness. The believer is taught that the delay of the enemy is the test, and the one who knows is the one whose awareness the Lord values, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose awareness the Lord accepts, and the answer of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the awareness is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose awareness the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the test of the delay: the enemy was granted the time, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose awareness the Lord values, and the answer is the teaching that the awareness is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the awareness that the test requires.",
  "81": "“until the appointed Day.”” completes the answer, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the answer: or \"ila yawmi al-waqti al-ma'loom,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord bounded the delay, and the completion is the teaching, the declaration of the term, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the term is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the teaching and the term is the bound, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own awareness. The believer is taught that the term of the enemy is the bound, and the one who knows is the one whose awareness the Lord values, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose awareness the Lord accepts, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the awareness is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose awareness the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the bound of the trial: the enemy's term is the appointed, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose awareness the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the awareness is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the awareness that the term requires.",
  "82": "“Satan said, “By Your Glory! I will certainly mislead them all,”” records the vow, and the commentators note the two parts of the vow. The verse records the vow: or \"qala fa-bi-'izzatika la-ughwiyannahum ajma'een,\" and the commentators note the vow, for Iblis vowed the misleading, and the vow is the enmity, the declaration of the war, and the verse is the record of the vow, the enmity that the following verse qualifies, and the commentators note the significance, for the vow is the enmity, and the verse is the record of the vow, the enmity of Iblis. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the vow is the enmity and the misleading is the war, and the verse is the record of the vow, the enmity that the following verse qualifies.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the vow, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own awareness. The believer is taught that the vow of the enemy is the war, and the one who knows is the one whose awareness the Lord values, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose awareness the Lord accepts, and the vow of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the awareness is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the vow is the believer whose awareness the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the war of the enemy: Iblis vowed the misleading, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose awareness the Lord values, and the vow is the teaching that the awareness is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the vow, and the teaching of the awareness that the war requires.",
  "83": "“except Your chosen servants among them.”” qualifies the vow, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse qualifies the vow: or \"illa 'ibadaka minhumu al-mukhlaseen,\" and the commentators note the qualification, for Iblis excepted the chosen, and the qualification is the confession, the declaration of the protection, and the verse is the record of the qualification, the confession that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the exception is the confession, and the verse is the record of the qualification, the confession of Iblis. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the qualification is the confession and the exception is the sincerity, and the verse is the record of the qualification, the confession that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the qualification, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own sincerity. The believer is taught that the sincerity is the shield, and the one who is sincere is the one whose protection the Lord grants, and the believer who holds the sincerity is the believer whose safety the Lord values, and the qualification of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the qualification is the believer whose sincerity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the shield of the sincerity: the chosen are protected, and the believer who holds the sincerity is the believer whose safety the Lord values, and the qualification is the teaching that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the qualification, and the teaching of the sincerity that the safety requires.",
  "84": "“Allah concluded, “The truth is—and I ˹only˺ say the truth—:”” records the conclusion, and the commentators note the two parts of the conclusion. The verse records the conclusion: or \"qala fal-haqqu wal-haqqa aqool,\" and the commentators note the conclusion, for the Lord declared the truth, and the conclusion is the teaching, the declaration of the truth, and the verse is the record of the conclusion, the teaching that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the truth is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the conclusion, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the conclusion is the teaching and the truth is the declaration, and the verse is the record of the conclusion, the teaching that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the conclusion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the word of the Lord is the truth, and the one who holds is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the word is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the conclusion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the conclusion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the truth of the Lord: the Lord declared the truth, and the believer who trusts the word is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the conclusion is the teaching that the truth is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the conclusion, and the teaching of the certainty that the truth produces.",
  "85": "“I will surely fill up Hell with you and whoever follows you from among them, all together.”” completes the conclusion, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the conclusion: or \"la-amla'anna jahannama minka wa mimman tabi'aka minhum ajma'een,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord declared the filling, and the completion is the judgment, the end of the followers, and the verse is the record of the completion, the judgment that the account closes with, and the commentators note the significance, for the filling is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the completion, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the judgment and the filling is the end, and the verse is the record of the completion, the judgment that the account closes with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own fear. The believer is taught that the judgment of the Lord is the thing that the believer fears, and the one who follows is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the Fire is filled, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the fear that the judgment produces.",
  "86": "“I do not ask you for any reward for this Quran, nor do I pretend to be someone I am not.” This verse records the Prophet’s declaration about his own motives and authenticity. He does not ask for any reward for the Quran. He is not seeking payment, benefit, or compensation for conveying the message. And he does not pretend to be someone he is not. He is not fabricating a role or claiming a status that is not his. The declaration establishes the purity of the Prophet’s motives and the authenticity of his self-presentation.\n\nThe significance of the declaration lies in its defense of the Prophet’s integrity. The claim to be a messenger of God, if false, might be motivated by the desire for reward, status, or power. The Prophet declares that he seeks none of these. He does not ask for reward, removing the most common motive for false religious claims. And he does not pretend to be someone he is not, establishing that his claim to prophethood is not a fabrication but a genuine communication of what he has been given. The two declarations together remove the most likely bases for suspicion about the Prophet’s motives and authenticity.\n\nThe phrase “I do not ask you for any reward” is a powerful testimony to the disinterestedness of the Prophet’s mission. A person who seeks reward may tailor his message to please his audience or to secure his compensation. A person who seeks no reward is free to deliver the message faithfully, regardless of the response. The Prophet’s freedom from the desire for reward is itself evidence of the genuineness of his calling: he conveys the message because it is his duty, not because it benefits him, and his faithfulness to the duty, regardless of the cost, testifies to the truth of his commission.\n\nThe phrase “nor do I pretend to be someone I am not” addresses the question of authenticity directly. The Prophet is not playing a role, fabricating an identity, or claiming a status that does not belong to him. What he presents himself to be, he is. The authenticity is crucial, because a messenger whose self-presentation is fabricated cannot be trusted in his message. The Prophet’s authenticity, established by his own declaration and confirmed by the consistent pattern of his life, supports the trustworthiness of the message he conveys.\n\nThe connection between the two declarations is significant. The absence of a desire for reward and the absence of pretense together present the Prophet as a faithful, genuine servant who conveys the message he has been given without self-serving motives and without false claims. The combination offers a complete defense of his integrity, addressing both the question of why he conveys the message, not for reward, and the question of who he is, not a pretender. The complete defense invites the listeners to receive the message with confidence in the messenger.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a testimony to the Prophet’s integrity and a call to receive his message with trust. It invites us to recognize that the Prophet sought no reward and fabricated no role, that his motives were pure and his self-presentation authentic, and that these qualities support the trustworthiness of the message he conveyed. The verse challenges us to receive the Quran with the confidence that its bearer was a faithful, genuine servant, and to examine our own motives and authenticity in whatever roles we bear, aspiring to the same freedom from self-serving desire and the same genuineness of self-presentation that characterized the Prophet, knowing that the integrity of the messenger is part of the evidence for the truth of the message he was commissioned to deliver.",
  "87": "“It is only a reminder to the whole world.” This verse identifies the Quran’s purpose and scope. It is a reminder, and its scope is the whole world. The identification defines what the Quran is, a reminder, and to whom it is addressed, the whole world. The two together present the Quran as a universal communication, a call to remembrance directed at all of humanity without exception.\n\nThe significance of the identification lies in its presentation of the Quran as a universal reminder. The Quran is not a message for one tribe, one nation, or one region but for the whole world. The universality of the scope reflects the universality of the need: all human beings, wherever they are, need the reminder that the Quran provides, the call to remember God, the truth, and the destiny that awaits. The universality also reflects the universality of God’s lordship: since God is the Lord of all, His reminder is addressed to all.\n\nThe word “reminder” identifies the nature of the Quran’s message. It is a reminder, a call to remember. The reminder assumes that there is something to be remembered, truths that human beings once knew or ought to know but tend to forget. The Quran does not introduce entirely foreign concepts but recalls the truths that are woven into the fabric of human existence: the oneness of God, the reality of accountability, the call to righteousness. The reminder reawakens these truths, bringing them back to the center of awareness where they belong.\n\nThe word “only” is significant. The Quran is “only” a reminder. The word does not diminish the Quran but clarifies its function. The Quran does not compel, does not force, does not override human freedom. It reminds. The reminding leaves the response to the hearer. The Quran does its work by bringing the truth to mind; what the hearer does with the truth is the hearer’s responsibility. The “only” defines the Quran’s role and, by implication, defines the limits of that role: the Quran reminds, and the choosing belongs to the human being.\n\nThe phrase “to the whole world” extends the Quran’s address beyond the immediate audience of the Prophet’s time and place. The Quran is not confined to seventh-century Arabia but speaks to the whole world, across time and space. The extension means that the Quran addresses every reader, in every age, as directly as it addressed its first hearers. The reminder is as fresh, as relevant, and as urgent for the reader today as it was for the listener then, because the truths it recalls are timeless and the need for them is universal.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an identification of the Quran’s purpose and scope and a call to receive it as a universal reminder. It invites us to recognize that the Quran is a reminder addressed to the whole world, including us, that its function is to call to mind the truths we need to remember, and that its reminding leaves the response to us. The verse challenges us to receive the Quran as the reminder it is, to allow it to reawaken the truths that we may have forgotten, and to respond to the reminder with the faith and action that the recalled truths demand, knowing that the Quran’s address to the whole world includes us, and that the reminder it offers is meant for our benefit, our guidance, and our eternal well-being.",
  "88": "“And you will certainly know its truth before long.” This verse declares the certainty that the truth of the Quran will be known. The addressees, who were turning away from the Quran, will certainly know its truth. The phrase “before long” suggests that the knowledge is not far off, that the reality the Quran proclaims will become evident, whether through the events of this life or, ultimately, on the Day of Judgment. The declaration is both a warning and a promise: the truth will be known, and those who deny it will discover their error.\n\nThe significance of the declaration lies in its certainty. The truth of the Quran will be known, certainly, without doubt. The certainty places the denial of the addressees in a tragic light: they are denying something whose truth they will inevitably recognize. The denial is temporary; the recognition is certain. The gap between the present denial and the future recognition is the gap of time, the interval during which the addressees persist in their refusal, before the truth they refused becomes undeniable.\n\nThe phrase “before long” suggests the nearness of the recognition. The addressees will not have to wait forever to know the truth of the Quran. The knowledge will come, and perhaps sooner than they expect. The nearness adds urgency to the warning: the time for denial is limited, and the recognition is approaching. The addressees who treat the Quran as false will, before long, know that it is true, and the knowledge, when it comes, will confirm what they denied.\n\nThe declaration serves as both a comfort and a warning. It comforts the Prophet and the believers by assuring them that the truth will be vindicated, that the denial of the opponents will not have the last word, that the Quran’s truth will be known. And it warns the deniers that their denial is futile, that the truth will catch up with them, and that the recognition they will gain will be a recognition of their error, not a confirmation of their denial. The dual function makes the declaration both encouraging and sobering.\n\nThe knowledge that will certainly come is the knowledge of the Quran’s truth. The Quran is true, regardless of whether it is recognized, but the recognition of its truth is also certain. The certainty rests on the character of God, who has declared it, and on the reality of the events the Quran proclaims, which will unfold and confirm the message. The deniers may refuse the recognition now, but they will not be able to refuse it forever. The truth will be known, and the denial will end.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a declaration of the certainty that the Quran’s truth will be known and a call to recognize it now rather than later. It invites us to acknowledge that the Quran is true, that its truth will be vindicated, and that the recognition of its truth is inevitable. The verse challenges us to receive the Quran’s truth now, in faith, before the recognition is forced upon us by the unfolding of the events the Quran proclaims. Knowing that the truth will certainly be known, we do well to align ourselves with it now, while the alignment is a matter of faith and choice, rather than waiting for the knowledge that will come before long and that will confirm, to those who denied, the truth they refused to receive when receiving could have saved them."
};
